1
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:32,150
Men, are you over 40?

2
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:34,940
When you wake up in the morning, do you feel tired and rundown?

3
00:02:35,030 --> 00:02:37,490
Do you have that listless feeling?

4
00:04:15,330 --> 00:04:17,540
Jefferies. Congratulations, Jeff.

5
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:20,380
For what? For getting rid of that cast.

6
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:22,420
Who said I was getting rid of it?

7
00:04:22,510 --> 00:04:23,970
This is Wednesday.

8
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:27,850
Seven weeks from the day you broke your leg. Yes or no?

9
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:33,560
Gunnison, how did you ever get to be such a big editor with such a small memory?

10
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:35,600
Thrift, industry and hard work.

11
00:04:35,690 --> 00:04:38,770
And catching the publisher with his secretary.

12
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:41,610
Did I get the wrong day? No.

13
00:04:41,690 --> 00:04:43,450
No, wrong week.

14
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:48,160
Next Wednesday I emerge from this plaster cocoon.

15
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:50,290
That's too bad, Jeff.

16
00:04:50,370 --> 00:04:53,160
Well, I guess I can't be lucky every day.

17
00:04:53,250 --> 00:04:54,580
Forget I called.

18
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:57,960
Yeah, I sure feel sorry for you, Gunnison.

19
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:01,670
Must be rough on you thinking of me wearing this cast for another whole week.

20
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:04,630
That one week is gonna cost me my best photographer

21
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:06,970
and you a big assignment.

22
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:09,850
Where? There's no point in even talking about it.

23
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:13,140
Oh, come on, come on. Where? Kashmir.

24
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:15,850
Got a code tip from the bureau chief this morning.

25
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:17,690
The place is about to go up in smoke.

26
00:05:17,770 --> 00:05:20,520
What did I tell you? Didn't I tell you that's the next place to watch?

27
00:05:20,610 --> 00:05:24,030
You did. Okay. When do I leave? Half-hour? Hour?

28
00:05:24,110 --> 00:05:28,660
With that cast on? You don't. Stop sounding stuffy.

29
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:32,620
I can take pictures from a jeep or a water buffalo, if necessary.

30
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:35,830
You're too valuable to the magazine for us to play around with.

31
00:05:35,910 --> 00:05:39,750
I'll send Morgan or Lambert. Morgan or Lambert. That's fine.

32
00:05:40,630 --> 00:05:42,960
I get myself half-killed for you,

33
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:45,590
and you reward me by stealing my assignments.

34
00:05:45,670 --> 00:05:49,340
I didn't ask you to stand in the middle of that automobile racetrack.

35
00:05:49,430 --> 00:05:53,970
You asked for something dramatically different. You got it.

36
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:56,480
So did you. Goodbye, Jeff.

37
00:05:56,560 --> 00:06:00,020
Now wait a minute, Gunnison. You've got to get me out of here.

38
00:06:00,110 --> 00:06:02,570
Six weeks sitting in a two-room apartment

39
00:06:02,650 --> 00:06:04,860
with nothing to do but look out the window at the neighbors.

40
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:07,360
Bye, Jeff. No, Gunnison, I...

41
00:06:07,450 --> 00:06:10,240
If you don't pull me out of this swamp of boredom,

42
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:12,160
I'm gonna do something drastic.

43
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:14,370
Like what? "Like what?" I'm gonna get married.

44
00:06:14,450 --> 00:06:16,710
And then I'll never be able to go anywhere.

45
00:06:16,790 --> 00:06:18,160
It's about time you got married,

46
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:21,540
before you turn into a lonesome and bitter old man.

47
00:06:22,790 --> 00:06:25,050
Yeah, can't you just see me?

48
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:28,090
Rushing home to a hot apartment

49
00:06:28,170 --> 00:06:32,140
to listen to the automatic laundry and the electric dishwasher

50
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:36,930
and the garbage disposal and the nagging wife.

51
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:40,560
Jeff, wives don't nag anymore, they discuss.

52
00:06:40,650 --> 00:06:43,230
Is that so? Is that so?

53
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:45,900
Well, maybe in the high-rent district they discuss.

54
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:48,150
In my neighborhood, they still nag.

55
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:52,660
Yeah? Well, you know best. I'll call you later, Jeff.

56
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:55,740
Yeah, have some good news the next time, huh?

57
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:51,840
Good morning. I said, "Good morning!"

58
00:07:51,930 --> 00:07:53,720
Oh, good morning.

59
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:14,530
Say, I wouldn't dig so deep if I were you.

60
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:18,330
You're giving them far too much water.

61
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:21,580
Why don't you shut up?

62
00:08:24,870 --> 00:08:26,080
Well!

63
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:29,420
I do declare.

64
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:33,340
New York State sentence for a Peeping Tom is six months in the workhouse.

65
00:08:33,430 --> 00:08:34,970
Oh, hello, Stella.

66
00:08:35,050 --> 00:08:37,680
And they got no windows in the workhouse.

67
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:41,770
You know, in the old days, they used to put your eyes out with a red-hot poker.

68
00:08:41,850 --> 00:08:46,770
Any of those bikini bombshells you're always watching worth a red-hot poker?

69
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:50,900
Oh, dear. We've become a race of Peeping Toms.

70
00:08:51,650 --> 00:08:55,860
What people ought to do is get outside their own house and look in for a change.

71
00:08:55,950 --> 00:08:57,280
Yes, sir.

72
00:08:57,370 --> 00:08:59,490
How's that for a bit of homespun philosophy?

73
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:02,040
Reader's Digest, April, 1939.

74
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:04,250
- Well, I only quote from the best. - Yeah.

75
00:09:04,330 --> 00:09:06,540
You don't have to take my temperature this morning.

76
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:09,380
Quiet. See if you can break 100.

77
00:09:09,460 --> 00:09:11,880
You know, I should have been a gypsy fortune-teller

78
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:14,050
instead of an insurance-company nurse.

79
00:09:14,130 --> 00:09:18,430
I got a nose for trouble. Can smell it ten miles away.

80
00:09:18,510 --> 00:09:22,890
You heard of that market crash in '29? I predicted that.

81
00:09:22,970 --> 00:09:25,100
Just how did you do that, Stella?

82
00:09:25,190 --> 00:09:26,480
Oh, simple.

83
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:30,070
I was nursing a director of General Motors.

84
00:09:30,150 --> 00:09:34,280
"Kidney ailment," they said. "Nerves," I said.

85
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:38,530
Then I asked myself, "What's General Motors got to be nervous about?"

86
00:09:39,490 --> 00:09:42,790
"Overproduction," I says. "Collapse."

87
00:09:42,870 --> 00:09:46,460
When General Motors has to go to the bathroom 10 times a day,

88
00:09:46,540 --> 00:09:49,080
the whole country's ready to let go.

89
00:09:50,790 --> 00:09:52,420
You know, Stella, in economics,

90
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:55,220
a kidney ailment has no relationship to the stock market.

91
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:58,590
None whatsoever. Crashed, didn't it?

92
00:09:58,680 --> 00:10:01,720
I can smell trouble right here in this apartment.

93
00:10:01,810 --> 00:10:06,390
First you smash your leg, then you get to looking out the window,

94
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:09,150
see things you shouldn't see. Trouble.

95
00:10:10,610 --> 00:10:15,940
I can see you in court now, surrounded by a bunch of lawyers in double-breasted suits.

96
00:10:16,030 --> 00:10:21,240
You're pleading. You say, "Judge, it was only a little bit of innocent fun.

97
00:10:21,320 --> 00:10:24,330
"I love my neighbors, like a father."

98
00:10:24,410 --> 00:10:26,450
And the judge says, "Well, congratulations.

99
00:10:26,540 --> 00:10:29,870
"You've just given birth to three years in Dannemora."

100
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:32,630
Right now I'd welcome trouble, you know.

101
00:10:32,710 --> 00:10:36,050
You've got a hormone deficiency. How can you tell from a thermometer?

102
00:10:36,130 --> 00:10:38,050
Those bathing beauties you've been watching

103
00:10:38,130 --> 00:10:41,590
haven't raised your temperature one degree in a month.

104
00:10:44,430 --> 00:10:45,810
Here we go.

105
00:10:51,650 --> 00:10:53,360
One more week.

106
00:10:57,240 --> 00:11:00,240
No, I think you're right. I think there is gonna be trouble around here.

107
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:01,740
I knew it.

108
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:05,410
Do you ever heat that stuff?

109
00:11:05,490 --> 00:11:08,330
Gives your circulation something to fight. Oh, I see.

110
00:11:08,410 --> 00:11:09,960
What kind of trouble?

111
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:13,080
Lisa Fremont. You kidding?

112
00:11:13,170 --> 00:11:17,840
She's a beautiful young girl, and you're a reasonably healthy young man.

113
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:20,340
She expects me to marry her. That's normal.

114
00:11:20,430 --> 00:11:22,720
I don't want to. That's abnormal.

115
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:25,430
I just... I'm not ready for marriage.

116
00:11:25,510 --> 00:11:28,680
Every man's ready for marriage when the right girl comes along.

117
00:11:28,770 --> 00:11:31,690
And Lisa Fremont is the right girl for any man with half a brain

118
00:11:31,770 --> 00:11:33,190
who can get one eye open.

119
00:11:33,270 --> 00:11:34,860
She's all right.

120
00:11:34,940 --> 00:11:36,860
- What'd you do, have a fight? - No.

121
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:39,650
Father loading up the shotgun? What?

122
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:42,530
Please, Stella. It's happened before, you know.

123
00:11:42,610 --> 00:11:44,160
Some of the world's happiest marriages

124
00:11:44,240 --> 00:11:47,740
have started "under the gun," as you might say.

125
00:11:47,830 --> 00:11:50,410
No, she's just not the girl for me. Yeah, she's only perfect.

126
00:11:50,500 --> 00:11:55,500
She's too perfect. She's too talented. She's too beautiful. She's too sophisticated.

127
00:11:55,590 --> 00:11:57,630
She's too everything but what I want.

128
00:11:57,710 --> 00:12:01,090
Is what you want something you can discuss?

129
00:12:01,170 --> 00:12:03,590
What? It's very simple, Stella.

130
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:07,760
She belongs to that rarefied atmosphere of Park Avenue, you know.

131
00:12:07,850 --> 00:12:11,020
Expensive restaurants and literary cocktail parties.

132
00:12:11,100 --> 00:12:13,140
People with sense belong wherever they're put.

133
00:12:13,230 --> 00:12:16,150
Can you imagine her traveling around the world with a camera bum

134
00:12:16,230 --> 00:12:18,320
who never has more than a week's salary in the bank?

135
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:21,070
If she was only ordinary.

136
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:23,450
You never gonna get married?

137
00:12:23,530 --> 00:12:25,990
I'll probably get married one of these days,

138
00:12:26,070 --> 00:12:28,530
but when I do, it's gonna be to someone who thinks of life

139
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:33,290
not just as a new dress and a lobster dinner

140
00:12:33,370 --> 00:12:35,420
or latest scandal.

141
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:38,500
I need a woman who's willing to... Hold it.

142
00:12:38,590 --> 00:12:42,470
Who's willing to go anywhere and do anything and love it.

143
00:12:43,260 --> 00:12:46,140
So the honest thing for me to do is just call the whole thing off.

144
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:50,220
Let her find somebody else. Yeah, I can hear you now.

145
00:12:50,310 --> 00:12:52,560
"Get out of my life, you perfectly wonderful woman.

146
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:54,310
"You're too good for me."

147
00:12:54,390 --> 00:12:56,770
Look, Mr. Jefferies, I'm not an educated woman,

148
00:12:56,850 --> 00:12:58,610
but I can tell you one thing.

149
00:12:58,690 --> 00:13:02,030
When a man and a woman see each other and like each other,

150
00:13:02,110 --> 00:13:06,450
they ought to come together, wham, like a couple of taxis on Broadway,

151
00:13:06,530 --> 00:13:10,740
and not sit around analyzing each other like two specimens in a bottle.

152
00:13:10,830 --> 00:13:13,960
There's an intelligent way to approach marriage.

153
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:15,160
Intelligence.

154
00:13:15,250 --> 00:13:19,340
Nothing has caused the human race so much trouble as intelligence.

155
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:22,010
Modern marriage.

156
00:13:22,090 --> 00:13:26,090
No, we've progressed emotionally. Baloney.

157
00:13:26,180 --> 00:13:29,510
Once it was, "See somebody, get excited, get married."

158
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:34,390
Now it's, "Read a lot of books, fence with a lot of four-syllable words,

159
00:13:34,480 --> 00:13:36,230
"psychoanalyze each other

160
00:13:36,310 --> 00:13:41,980
"until you can't tell the difference between "a petting party and a civil service exam."

161
00:13:42,070 --> 00:13:44,530
People have different emotional levels...

162
00:13:44,610 --> 00:13:48,490
When I married Myles, we were both a couple of maladjusted misfits.

163
00:13:48,570 --> 00:13:54,000
We are still maladjusted misfits, and we have loved every minute of it.

164
00:13:54,080 --> 00:13:55,080
Well, that's fine, Stella.

165
00:13:55,160 --> 00:13:58,460
Now, would you fix me a sandwich, please? Yes, I will.

166
00:13:58,540 --> 00:14:01,420
And I'll spread a little common sense on the bread.

167
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:04,340
Lisa's loaded to her fingertips with love for you.

168
00:14:04,420 --> 00:14:07,470
I got two words of advice for you. Marry her.

169
00:14:07,550 --> 00:14:09,930
She pay you much? Oh!

170
00:14:26,780 --> 00:14:28,320
There.

171
00:14:32,410 --> 00:14:34,490
- Here's the key. - Thank you.

172
00:14:42,290 --> 00:14:45,170
Well, if you want anything, just ring.

173
00:14:57,980 --> 00:15:00,310
Honey. Come on.

174
00:15:07,900 --> 00:15:10,490
Got to carry you over the threshold.

175
00:15:33,510 --> 00:15:35,100
Window shopper.

176
00:16:25,230 --> 00:16:28,570
How's your leg? It hurts a little.

177
00:16:29,230 --> 00:16:32,240
And your stomach? Empty as a football.

178
00:16:34,910 --> 00:16:38,580
And your love life? Not too active.

179
00:16:40,160 --> 00:16:42,290
Anything else bothering you?

180
00:16:44,540 --> 00:16:46,170
Who are you?

181
00:16:48,630 --> 00:16:52,670
Reading from top to bottom, Lisa

182
00:16:56,180 --> 00:16:57,510
Carol

183
00:17:01,850 --> 00:17:03,140
Fremont.

184
00:17:04,730 --> 00:17:08,190
Is this the Lisa Fremont who never wears the same dress twice?

185
00:17:08,270 --> 00:17:10,730
Only because it's expected of her.

186
00:17:10,820 --> 00:17:14,570
It's right off the Paris plane. Do you think it'll sell?

187
00:17:14,650 --> 00:17:17,200
That depends on the quote, you know. Let's see now.

188
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:22,040
There's the airplane ticket over and import duties, hidden taxes, profit markup...

189
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:26,540
A steal at $1,100. Eleven hundred?

190
00:17:26,620 --> 00:17:29,040
They ought to list that dress on the stock exchange.

191
00:17:29,130 --> 00:17:32,210
Why, we sell a dozen a day in this price range.

192
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:33,710
Who buys them, tax collectors?

193
00:17:33,800 --> 00:17:37,300
Even if I had to pay, it would be worth it. Just for the occasion.

194
00:17:37,390 --> 00:17:39,510
Why, is something big going on somewhere?

195
00:17:39,600 --> 00:17:42,390
It's going on right here. It's a big night.

196
00:17:42,470 --> 00:17:47,810
It's just an old run-of-the-mill Wednesday. The calendar's full of them.

197
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:52,900
It's opening night of the last depressing week of L. B. Jefferies in a cast.

198
00:17:52,980 --> 00:17:56,610
Well, I haven't noticed a big demand for tickets or anything.

199
00:17:56,700 --> 00:17:59,490
That's because I bought out the house.

200
00:17:59,570 --> 00:18:02,370
You know, this cigarette box has seen better days.

201
00:18:02,450 --> 00:18:07,000
I picked that up in Shanghai, which has also seen better days.

202
00:18:07,080 --> 00:18:11,380
It's cracked and you never use it. It's too ornate.

203
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:15,340
I'm sending up a plain, flat, silver one with just your initials engraved.

204
00:18:15,420 --> 00:18:18,090
That's no way to spend your hard-earned money.

205
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:19,590
Well, I wanted to.

206
00:18:23,350 --> 00:18:26,430
What would you think of starting off with dinner at '21'?

207
00:18:26,520 --> 00:18:29,060
You have, perhaps, an ambulance downstairs?

208
00:18:29,150 --> 00:18:32,690
No, better than that. '21.'

209
00:18:37,150 --> 00:18:40,740
Thank you for waiting, Carl. The kitchen's right there on the left.

210
00:18:40,820 --> 00:18:42,580
Oh, I'll take the wine.

211
00:18:45,290 --> 00:18:47,080
- Good evening, Mr. Jefferies. - Carl.

212
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:49,790
Just put everything in the oven, Carl, on low.

213
00:18:49,870 --> 00:18:51,500
- Yes, madam. - Let's open the wine now.

214
00:18:51,580 --> 00:18:52,960
All right.

215
00:18:53,710 --> 00:18:55,170
It's a Montrachet.

216
00:18:55,250 --> 00:18:58,380
A great big glassful. There's a corkscrew right over there.

217
00:19:00,180 --> 00:19:01,890
Here, I'll do it.

218
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:06,180
Big enough? Yeah, they're fine.

219
00:19:06,270 --> 00:19:10,140
I can't think of anything more boring or tiresome than what you've been through,

220
00:19:10,230 --> 00:19:11,980
- and the last week must be the hardest. - Let me, sir.

221
00:19:12,060 --> 00:19:13,650
All right.

222
00:19:13,730 --> 00:19:16,820
Yeah, I want to get this thing off and get moving.

223
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:20,400
Well, I'm going to make this a week you'll never forget.

224
00:19:22,910 --> 00:19:24,950
Fine, thanks.

225
00:19:25,030 --> 00:19:27,330
Just a minute, Carl.

226
00:19:27,410 --> 00:19:30,660
This will take care of the taxi as well. Thank you, Miss Fremont.

227
00:19:30,750 --> 00:19:34,630
Have a pleasant dinner, Mr. Jefferies. All right. Good night.

228
00:19:34,710 --> 00:19:36,090
Good night.

229
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:39,840
What a day I've had.

230
00:19:39,920 --> 00:19:42,090
Are you tired? Not a bit.

231
00:19:42,180 --> 00:19:44,350
I was all morning in a sales meeting,

232
00:19:44,430 --> 00:19:47,930
then I had to dash to the Waldorf for a quick drink with Madame Dufresne,

233
00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:51,190
who's just over from Paris with some spy reports.

234
00:19:52,350 --> 00:19:56,320
And then I had to go to '21 ' and have lunch with the Harper's Bazaar people.

235
00:19:56,440 --> 00:19:58,400
And that's when I ordered dinner.

236
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:01,200
Then I had two fall showings 20 blocks apart.

237
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:03,860
Then I had to have a cocktail with Leland and Slim Hayward.

238
00:20:03,950 --> 00:20:05,700
We're trying to get his new show.

239
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:07,830
And then I had to dash back and change.

240
00:20:07,910 --> 00:20:12,500
Well, now, tell me. Tell me. Now, what was Mrs. Hayward wearing?

241
00:20:12,580 --> 00:20:14,420
- Oh, she looked wonderfully cool. - She did.

242
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:17,420
She had on the most divine Italian hand-print

243
00:20:17,500 --> 00:20:19,510
- mousseline de soie... - Oh, Italian?

244
00:20:19,590 --> 00:20:21,550
- Oh, you. - Italian. Imagine.

245
00:20:21,630 --> 00:20:25,140
To think I planted three nice items in the columns about you today.

246
00:20:25,220 --> 00:20:27,720
You did? You can't buy that kind of publicity.

247
00:20:27,810 --> 00:20:29,390
I know.

248
00:20:29,470 --> 00:20:33,390
Someday you may want to open up a studio of your own here.

249
00:20:33,480 --> 00:20:37,150
How would I run it from, say, Pakistan?

250
00:20:37,820 --> 00:20:39,480
Jeff...

251
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:44,780
Isn't it time you came home? You could pick your assignment.

252
00:20:45,820 --> 00:20:49,080
I wish there was one I wanted. Make the one you want.

253
00:20:49,160 --> 00:20:50,910
- You mean leave the magazine? - Yes.

254
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,960
For what? For yourself and me.

255
00:20:55,540 --> 00:20:57,290
I could get you a dozen assignments tomorrow.

256
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:03,260
Don't laugh. I could do it. That's what I'm afraid of.

257
00:21:03,340 --> 00:21:06,550
Can you see me driving down to the fashion salon in a jeep,

258
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:09,760
wearing combat boots and a three-day beard?

259
00:21:09,850 --> 00:21:11,220
Wouldn't that make a hit?

260
00:21:11,350 --> 00:21:15,980
I could see you looking very handsome and successful in a dark blue flannel suit.

261
00:21:16,060 --> 00:21:19,690
Let's stop talking nonsense, shall we?

262
00:21:23,440 --> 00:21:26,450
Guess I'd better start setting up for dinner.

263
00:23:30,820 --> 00:23:32,490
Miss Lonelyheart.

264
00:23:32,570 --> 00:23:35,410
At least that's something you'll never have to worry about.

265
00:23:35,490 --> 00:23:37,080
Oh?

266
00:23:37,160 --> 00:23:41,040
You can see my apartment from here, all the way up on 63rd Street?

267
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:44,290
No, not exactly, but we have a little apartment here

268
00:23:44,380 --> 00:23:46,590
that's probably about as popular as yours.

269
00:23:46,670 --> 00:23:50,260
You remember, of course, Miss Torso, the ballet dancer?

270
00:23:51,300 --> 00:23:54,550
She's like a queen bee with her pick of the drones.

271
00:24:04,230 --> 00:24:07,980
I'd say she's doing a woman's hardest job. Juggling wolves.

272
00:24:15,030 --> 00:24:16,370
Thank you.

273
00:24:28,460 --> 00:24:31,300
Well, she picked the most prosperous-looking one.

274
00:24:31,380 --> 00:24:34,470
She's not in love with him or any of them.

275
00:24:34,550 --> 00:24:37,140
Oh, how can you tell that from here?

276
00:24:38,220 --> 00:24:41,430
You said it resembled my apartment, didn't you?

277
00:24:57,740 --> 00:25:00,080
I hope they're cooked this time.

278
00:26:25,540 --> 00:26:27,920
Where's that wonderful music coming from?

279
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:31,290
Some songwriter over there in the studio apartment.

280
00:26:32,800 --> 00:26:36,840
He lives alone. Probably had a very unhappy marriage.

281
00:26:38,550 --> 00:26:40,340
Oh, it's enchanting.

282
00:26:43,640 --> 00:26:47,270
It's almost as if it were being written especially for us.

283
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:51,480
No wonder he's having so much trouble with it.

284
00:26:54,190 --> 00:26:56,820
At least you can't say the dinner isn't right.

285
00:26:56,900 --> 00:27:00,030
Lisa, it's perfect.

286
00:27:02,120 --> 00:27:03,530
As always.

287
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:17,800
There can't be that much difference between people and the way they live.

288
00:27:17,880 --> 00:27:20,380
We all eat, talk, drink, laugh, wear clothes.

289
00:27:20,470 --> 00:27:21,930
Well, now look...

290
00:27:22,010 --> 00:27:24,810
If you're saying all this because you don't want to tell me the truth,

291
00:27:24,890 --> 00:27:27,270
because you're hiding something from me, then maybe I can understand.

292
00:27:27,350 --> 00:27:30,480
I'm not hiding anything. It's just that... It doesn't make sense.

293
00:27:30,560 --> 00:27:33,650
What's so different about it here from over there or anyplace you go

294
00:27:33,730 --> 00:27:36,320
that one person couldn't live in both places just as easily?

295
00:27:36,400 --> 00:27:37,990
Some people can. Now, if you'll just let me...

296
00:27:38,070 --> 00:27:41,450
What is it but traveling from one place to another, taking pictures?

297
00:27:41,530 --> 00:27:43,700
It's just like being a tourist on an endless vacation.

298
00:27:43,780 --> 00:27:45,120
Okay. Now that's your opinion.

299
00:27:45,200 --> 00:27:46,990
You're entitled to it. Now, let me give you my side.

300
00:27:47,080 --> 00:27:49,160
It's ridiculous to say that it can only be done

301
00:27:49,250 --> 00:27:52,290
by a special, private little group of anointed people.

302
00:27:52,370 --> 00:27:55,290
I made a simple statement, a true statement,

303
00:27:55,380 --> 00:27:58,880
but I can back it up if you'd just shut up for a minute.

304
00:27:58,960 --> 00:28:02,300
If your opinion is as rude as your manner, I don't think I care to hear it.

305
00:28:02,380 --> 00:28:03,800
Oh, come on now. Simmer down.

306
00:28:03,890 --> 00:28:07,180
I can't fit in here. You can't fit in there.

307
00:28:07,260 --> 00:28:10,520
I mean, according to you, people should be born, live and die on the same spot!

308
00:28:10,600 --> 00:28:11,810
Shut up!

309
00:28:14,560 --> 00:28:18,650
Did you ever eat fish heads and rice? Of course not.

310
00:28:18,730 --> 00:28:21,280
Well, you might have to if you went with me.

311
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:27,120
Did you ever try to keep warm in a C-54 at 15,000 feet, 20 degrees below zero?

312
00:28:27,200 --> 00:28:30,370
Oh, I do it all the time. Whenever I have a few minutes after lunch.

313
00:28:30,450 --> 00:28:32,920
Did you ever get shot at? Did you ever get run over?

314
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:34,420
Did you ever get sandbagged at night

315
00:28:34,500 --> 00:28:37,790
because somebody got unfavorable publicity from your camera?

316
00:28:37,880 --> 00:28:40,670
Did you ever... Those high heels, they'll be great in the jungle.

317
00:28:40,760 --> 00:28:42,930
And the nylons and those six-ounce lingerie.

318
00:28:43,010 --> 00:28:44,550
- Three. - All right, three.

319
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:48,060
They'll make a big hit in Finland, just before you freeze to death.

320
00:28:48,140 --> 00:28:51,730
Well, if there's one thing I know, it's how to wear the proper clothes.

321
00:28:51,810 --> 00:28:52,980
Yeah, yeah.

322
00:28:53,060 --> 00:28:56,610
Well, try and find a raincoat in Brazil, even when it isn't raining.

323
00:28:56,690 --> 00:29:00,320
Lisa, in this job you carry one suitcase.

324
00:29:00,400 --> 00:29:02,900
Your home is the available transportation.

325
00:29:02,990 --> 00:29:05,160
You don't sleep very much. You bathe less.

326
00:29:05,240 --> 00:29:07,280
And sometimes the food that you eat

327
00:29:07,370 --> 00:29:11,120
is made from things that you couldn't even look at when they're alive.

328
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:15,000
Jeff, you don't have to be deliberately repulsive just to impress me I'm wrong.

329
00:29:15,080 --> 00:29:18,420
Deliberately repulsive? I'm just trying to make it sound good.

330
00:29:18,500 --> 00:29:21,760
You just have to face it, Lisa, you're not meant for that kind of a life.

331
00:29:21,840 --> 00:29:23,550
Few people are.

332
00:29:25,380 --> 00:29:30,430
You're too stubborn to argue with. I'm not stubborn. I'm just truthful.

333
00:29:30,510 --> 00:29:34,390
I know, a lesser man would have told me it was one long holiday,

334
00:29:34,480 --> 00:29:38,190
and I would have been awakened to a rude disillusionment.

335
00:29:38,270 --> 00:29:40,570
Oh, well now, wait a minute. Wait a minute.

336
00:29:40,650 --> 00:29:45,490
If you want to get vicious on this thing, I'd be very happy to accommodate you.

337
00:29:46,860 --> 00:29:49,740
No, I don't particularly want that.

338
00:29:52,120 --> 00:29:53,620
So that's it?

339
00:29:53,700 --> 00:29:56,830
You won't stay here, and I can't go with you.

340
00:29:56,920 --> 00:29:59,040
It would be the wrong thing.

341
00:30:01,210 --> 00:30:05,220
You don't think either one of us could ever change?

342
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:07,510
Right now it doesn't seem so.

343
00:30:12,560 --> 00:30:16,390
I'm in love with you. I don't care what you do for a living.

344
00:30:17,730 --> 00:30:20,310
I'd just like to be part of it somehow.

345
00:30:22,570 --> 00:30:25,530
It's deflating to find out the only way I can be part of it

346
00:30:25,610 --> 00:30:28,740
is to take out a subscription to your magazine.

347
00:30:30,280 --> 00:30:33,080
I guess I'm not the girl I thought I was.

348
00:30:33,740 --> 00:30:35,160
There's nothing wrong with you, Lisa.

349
00:30:35,250 --> 00:30:38,250
You've got this town in the palm of your hand.

350
00:30:40,130 --> 00:30:41,920
Not quite, it seems.

351
00:30:44,050 --> 00:30:47,010
Goodbye, Jeff. You mean good night.

352
00:30:47,090 --> 00:30:48,430
I mean what I said.

353
00:30:48,510 --> 00:30:51,930
Well, Lisa, couldn't we just...

354
00:30:53,930 --> 00:30:58,020
Couldn't we just keep things status quo?

355
00:31:00,850 --> 00:31:02,520
Without any future?

356
00:31:02,610 --> 00:31:04,940
When am I gonna see you again?

357
00:31:06,570 --> 00:31:08,490
Not for a long time.

358
00:31:10,410 --> 00:31:11,620
At least

359
00:31:11,740 --> 00:31:13,830
not until tomorrow night.

360
00:31:50,320 --> 00:31:51,490
Don't!

361
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:18,520
Hey! Rain.

362
00:32:22,350 --> 00:32:25,980
Grab that. The rest of the blanket. Wait, I'll get the clock.

363
00:32:26,060 --> 00:32:27,400
Oh, no!

364
00:32:30,360 --> 00:32:31,610
Put it in, now.

365
00:32:35,370 --> 00:32:36,910
Pull on it.

366
00:32:36,990 --> 00:32:38,950
Let me do it.

367
00:32:39,040 --> 00:32:40,700
In you go.

368
00:34:57,170 --> 00:35:00,680
No, you can't come in. It's much too late.

369
00:35:00,760 --> 00:35:05,100
No, no, not now. Get your foot out of the way. No, I said good night.

370
00:36:46,070 --> 00:36:49,870
What's that supposed to be, ma'am? It's called "Hunger."

371
00:37:13,310 --> 00:37:16,190
You'd think the rain would have cooled things off.

372
00:37:16,270 --> 00:37:18,570
All it did was make the heat wet. That's it. That's it.

373
00:37:18,650 --> 00:37:20,650
That's stiff one, right there. Right there.

374
00:37:20,730 --> 00:37:23,150
You know, the insurance company would be much happier

375
00:37:23,240 --> 00:37:26,280
if you'd sleep in bed at night instead of in that wheelchair.

376
00:37:26,360 --> 00:37:29,200
How'd you know? Eyes are all bloodshot.

377
00:37:29,280 --> 00:37:31,240
Must have been watching out that window for hours.

378
00:37:31,330 --> 00:37:32,790
Yeah, I was.

379
00:37:32,870 --> 00:37:34,460
What are you gonna do if one of them catches you?

380
00:37:34,540 --> 00:37:36,080
It depends which one.

381
00:37:36,170 --> 00:37:39,920
Miss Torso, for example... You keep your mind off her.

382
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:42,460
She sure is the "Eat, drink and be merry" girl.

383
00:37:42,550 --> 00:37:45,720
Yeah, she'll wind up fat, alcoholic and miserable.

384
00:37:45,800 --> 00:37:47,090
Yeah.

385
00:37:47,180 --> 00:37:53,310
Speaking of misery, poor Miss Lonelyheart. She drank herself to sleep again, alone.

386
00:37:53,390 --> 00:37:55,600
Poor soul. Oh, well.

387
00:37:55,690 --> 00:37:57,690
Maybe one day she'll find her happiness.

388
00:37:57,770 --> 00:38:00,610
Yeah, and some man will lose his.

389
00:38:00,690 --> 00:38:04,280
Isn't there anybody in the neighborhood who could cast an eye in her direction?

390
00:38:04,360 --> 00:38:05,740
I don't know.

391
00:38:05,820 --> 00:38:09,530
It just might be that the salesman will be available soon.

392
00:38:09,740 --> 00:38:13,290
Oh, him and his wife splitting up? I just can't figure it.

393
00:38:13,370 --> 00:38:18,040
He went out several times last night in the rain, carrying his sample case.

394
00:38:18,120 --> 00:38:19,330
Well, he's a salesman, isn't he?

395
00:38:19,420 --> 00:38:21,800
What would he be selling at 3:00 in the morning?

396
00:38:21,880 --> 00:38:23,300
Flashlights.

397
00:38:23,380 --> 00:38:27,550
Luminous dials for watches. House numbers that light up.

398
00:38:27,630 --> 00:38:30,850
I don't think so. I don't think so.

399
00:38:31,680 --> 00:38:35,060
I think he was taking something out of the apartment. Uh-huh.

400
00:38:36,350 --> 00:38:38,140
His personal effects.

401
00:38:39,060 --> 00:38:43,150
He's gonna run out on her, the coward. Yeah.

402
00:38:43,230 --> 00:38:45,900
Sometimes it's worse to stay than it is to run.

403
00:38:45,990 --> 00:38:50,820
Yeah, well, it takes a particularly low type of man to do a thing like that.

404
00:38:52,450 --> 00:38:55,620
How about this morning? Any further developments?

405
00:38:56,660 --> 00:38:58,750
The shades are all drawn in the apartment.

406
00:38:58,870 --> 00:39:00,880
- In this heat? - Yeah.

407
00:39:00,960 --> 00:39:02,790
Well, they're up now.

408
00:39:08,590 --> 00:39:11,340
Get back! Get back! Where do you want me to go?

409
00:39:11,180 --> 00:39:14,220
Come on, get out of sight. Get out of sight. What is it? What's the matter?

410
00:39:14,310 --> 00:39:17,020
That salesman's looking out of his window. You see?

411
00:39:17,100 --> 00:39:19,060
Get back! He'll see you.

412
00:39:19,140 --> 00:39:21,600
I'm not shy. I've been looked at before.

413
00:39:21,690 --> 00:39:23,650
That's no ordinary look.

414
00:39:25,280 --> 00:39:27,030
That's the kind of look a man gives

415
00:39:27,110 --> 00:39:30,280
when he's afraid somebody might be watching him.

416
00:39:46,960 --> 00:39:49,420
Get away from there. Get away from there. He'll be after you.

417
00:39:49,510 --> 00:39:52,140
Go on, into the house. Into the house.

418
00:39:54,510 --> 00:39:57,350
Goodbye, Mr. Jefferies. See you tomorrow.

419
00:40:07,150 --> 00:40:10,400
And don't sleep in that chair again. Uh-huh.

420
00:40:11,950 --> 00:40:13,950
Great conversationalist.

421
00:40:16,370 --> 00:40:17,660
Stella.

422
00:40:18,790 --> 00:40:22,620
Take those binoculars out of the case and bring them here, will you?

423
00:40:22,710 --> 00:40:25,540
Trouble. I can smell it.

424
00:40:26,920 --> 00:40:30,920
I'll be glad when they crack that cast, and I can get out of here. Uh-huh.

425
00:44:07,270 --> 00:44:10,520
How far does a girl have to go before you'll notice her?

426
00:44:10,600 --> 00:44:14,650
Well, if she's pretty enough, she doesn't have to go anywhere.

427
00:44:14,730 --> 00:44:18,070
She just has to "be." Well, ain't I?

428
00:44:18,820 --> 00:44:20,650
Pay attention to me.

429
00:44:23,200 --> 00:44:26,780
I'm not exactly on the other side of the room.

430
00:44:27,240 --> 00:44:28,830
Your mind is...

431
00:44:29,750 --> 00:44:32,580
And when I want a man, I want all of him.

432
00:44:36,960 --> 00:44:40,380
Don't you ever have any problems? I have one now.

433
00:44:41,920 --> 00:44:43,180
So do I.

434
00:44:44,760 --> 00:44:46,640
Tell me about it.

435
00:44:48,930 --> 00:44:50,100
Why...

436
00:44:51,350 --> 00:44:57,020
Why would a man leave his apartment three times on a rainy night with a suitcase,

437
00:44:57,110 --> 00:44:59,860
and come back three times?

438
00:44:59,940 --> 00:45:01,780
He likes the way his wife welcomes him home.

439
00:45:01,860 --> 00:45:03,780
No, no. No, no.

440
00:45:03,860 --> 00:45:06,870
No, no. Not this salesman's wife.

441
00:45:06,950 --> 00:45:11,370
And why didn't he go to work today? Homework. It's more interesting.

442
00:45:14,040 --> 00:45:17,540
What's interesting about a butcher knife

443
00:45:17,630 --> 00:45:20,050
and a small saw

444
00:45:20,130 --> 00:45:22,550
wrapped in newspaper? Hmm?

445
00:45:22,630 --> 00:45:25,220
Nothing, thank heaven.

446
00:45:25,300 --> 00:45:28,180
Why hasn't he been in his wife's bedroom all day?

447
00:45:28,300 --> 00:45:30,430
I wouldn't dare answer that.

448
00:45:31,810 --> 00:45:37,270
Well, listen. I'll answer it, Lisa. There's something terribly wrong.

449
00:45:40,150 --> 00:45:43,570
And I'm afraid it's with me. What do you think?

450
00:45:44,990 --> 00:45:47,490
Something too frightful to utter.

451
00:45:49,120 --> 00:45:53,330
He went out a few minutes ago in his undershirt, hasn't come back yet.

452
00:46:05,680 --> 00:46:08,680
That'd be a terrible job to tackle.

453
00:46:14,640 --> 00:46:17,730
Just how would you start to cut up a human body?

454
00:46:20,190 --> 00:46:24,400
Jeff, I'll be honest with you. You're beginning to scare me a little.

455
00:46:26,860 --> 00:46:31,200
Jeff, did you hear what I said? You're beginning to scare... Shh! Shh!

456
00:46:31,280 --> 00:46:32,990
He's coming back!

457
00:47:00,860 --> 00:47:03,860
Jeff, if you could only see yourself! What's the matter?

458
00:47:03,940 --> 00:47:06,530
Sitting around looking out of the window to kill time is one thing,

459
00:47:06,610 --> 00:47:10,700
but doing it the way you are, with binoculars and wild opinions

460
00:47:10,780 --> 00:47:13,410
about every little thing you see, is diseased!

461
00:47:13,490 --> 00:47:16,160
What, do you think I consider it recreation?

462
00:47:16,250 --> 00:47:18,000
I don't know what you consider it,

463
00:47:18,080 --> 00:47:20,170
but if you don't stop it, I'm getting out of here.

464
00:47:20,250 --> 00:47:22,790
What's the... What is it you're looking for?

465
00:47:22,880 --> 00:47:25,670
I just want to find out what's the matter with the salesman's wife, that's all.

466
00:47:25,760 --> 00:47:27,420
Does that make me sound like a madman?

467
00:47:27,510 --> 00:47:29,340
What makes you think there's something the matter with her?

468
00:47:29,430 --> 00:47:33,010
A lot of things. She's an invalid. She demands constant care.

469
00:47:33,100 --> 00:47:37,350
Yet neither the husband or anybody else has been in to see her all day. Why?

470
00:47:39,100 --> 00:47:40,440
Maybe she died.

471
00:47:40,520 --> 00:47:43,190
Where's the doctor? Where's the undertaker?

472
00:47:43,270 --> 00:47:46,150
She could be sleeping, under sedatives.

473
00:47:46,230 --> 00:47:49,070
He's in there now. There's nothing to see.

474
00:47:49,150 --> 00:47:51,660
There is something... I've seen it through that window.

475
00:47:51,740 --> 00:47:54,580
I've seen bickering and family quarrels

476
00:47:54,660 --> 00:47:57,910
and mysterious trips at night and knives and saws and ropes.

477
00:47:58,000 --> 00:47:59,870
And now, since last evening, not a sign of the wife.

478
00:47:59,960 --> 00:48:01,370
All right. Now you tell me where she is.

479
00:48:01,460 --> 00:48:03,380
- I don't know. - What's she doing? Where is she?

480
00:48:03,460 --> 00:48:06,420
Maybe he's leaving his wife. I don't know. I don't care!

481
00:48:06,500 --> 00:48:09,630
Lots of people have knives and saws and ropes around their houses.

482
00:48:09,720 --> 00:48:12,680
And lots of men don't speak to their wives all day.

483
00:48:12,760 --> 00:48:15,350
Lots of wives nag and men hate them and trouble starts,

484
00:48:15,430 --> 00:48:19,560
but very, very few of them end up in murder, if that's what you're thinking.

485
00:48:19,640 --> 00:48:22,020
It's pretty hard for you to keep away from that word, isn't it?

486
00:48:22,100 --> 00:48:24,770
- You could see all that he did, couldn't you? - Of course...

487
00:48:24,860 --> 00:48:26,480
You could see because the shades were up

488
00:48:26,570 --> 00:48:29,860
and he walked along the corridor and the street and the backyard.

489
00:48:29,940 --> 00:48:31,320
I've seen him in the backyard, fixing the flowers...

490
00:48:31,400 --> 00:48:34,410
Oh, Jeff, do you think a murderer would let you see all that?

491
00:48:34,490 --> 00:48:37,200
That he wouldn't pull the shades down and hide behind them?

492
00:48:37,280 --> 00:48:39,410
Just where he's being clever. He's being nonchalant about it.

493
00:48:39,500 --> 00:48:41,330
And that's where you're not being clever.

494
00:48:41,410 --> 00:48:45,250
A murderer would never parade his crime in front of an open window.

495
00:48:45,330 --> 00:48:46,420
Why not?

496
00:48:46,500 --> 00:48:47,500
Why, for all you know,

497
00:48:47,590 --> 00:48:50,760
there's probably something a lot more sinister going on behind those windows.

498
00:48:50,840 --> 00:48:52,130
Where?

499
00:48:55,800 --> 00:48:57,220
No comment.

500
00:49:19,830 --> 00:49:23,410
Let's start from the beginning again, Jeff.

501
00:49:23,500 --> 00:49:26,880
Tell me everything you saw

502
00:49:26,960 --> 00:49:29,750
and what you think it means.

503
00:49:42,220 --> 00:49:44,390
Yeah? The name on the second floor

504
00:49:44,440 --> 00:49:47,980
rear mail box reads, "Mr. and Mrs. Lars."

505
00:49:48,060 --> 00:49:50,400
That's L-A-R-S.

506
00:49:50,480 --> 00:49:52,490
Lars Thorwald.

507
00:49:52,570 --> 00:49:54,200
What's the number of the apartment house?

508
00:49:54,280 --> 00:49:56,820
125 West 9th Street.

509
00:49:58,070 --> 00:49:59,450
Thank you, dear.

510
00:49:59,530 --> 00:50:02,910
Okay, chief. What's my next assignment? Just go home.

511
00:50:03,790 --> 00:50:06,670
All right, but what's he doing now?

512
00:50:06,750 --> 00:50:10,210
He's just sitting in the living room in the dark.

513
00:50:11,000 --> 00:50:13,550
Hasn't gone near the bedroom.

514
00:50:13,630 --> 00:50:15,340
Now you go home and get some sleep.

515
00:50:15,420 --> 00:50:17,340
- Good night. - Good night.

516
00:50:27,270 --> 00:50:28,560
All right. What's it all about, Jeff?

517
00:50:28,650 --> 00:50:31,980
Now look, Doyle, it's just something I can't tell you over the telephone.

518
00:50:32,070 --> 00:50:33,940
You have to be here and see the whole setup.

519
00:50:34,030 --> 00:50:36,610
Important, huh? Well, it's probably nothing important at all.

520
00:50:36,700 --> 00:50:38,780
It's just a little neighborhood murder, that's all.

521
00:50:38,860 --> 00:50:41,580
Did you say "murder," Jeff? Yes, as a matter of fact, I did say "murder."

522
00:50:41,660 --> 00:50:43,040
Oh, come now.

523
00:50:43,120 --> 00:50:46,460
My only thought was throwing a little business your way, that's all.

524
00:50:46,540 --> 00:50:49,540
I figured a good detective would jump at the chance of something to detect.

525
00:50:49,630 --> 00:50:51,210
- I'm not working. - What?

526
00:50:51,290 --> 00:50:52,960
This happens to be my day off.

527
00:50:53,050 --> 00:50:56,010
Well, I usually took my best pictures on my day off.

528
00:50:56,130 --> 00:50:59,890
I'll drop by. Okay, Doyle. All right. As soon as you can.

529
00:51:01,300 --> 00:51:05,100
Bless your heart, Stella. Gee whiz, look at this.

530
00:51:05,180 --> 00:51:09,230
I can't tell you what a welcome sight this is. No wonder your husband still loves you.

531
00:51:09,310 --> 00:51:10,900
- The police. - What?

532
00:51:10,980 --> 00:51:13,980
You called the police. Well, not exactly.

533
00:51:14,070 --> 00:51:17,780
Not an official call. It's just an old friend of mine.

534
00:51:17,860 --> 00:51:20,910
An old, ornery friend of mine.

535
00:51:20,990 --> 00:51:23,830
Just where do you suppose he cut her up?

536
00:51:25,490 --> 00:51:28,000
Of course. The bathtub.

537
00:51:28,870 --> 00:51:32,920
That's the only place where he could have washed away the blood.

538
00:51:41,800 --> 00:51:45,680
He better get that trunk out of there before it starts to leak.

539
00:52:07,200 --> 00:52:08,620
Harry?

540
00:52:14,000 --> 00:52:16,050
Look, look, Mr. Jefferies.

541
00:52:53,750 --> 00:52:56,000
I thought Doyle would be here by the time the trunk left,

542
00:52:56,090 --> 00:52:58,590
or I'd have called the police. Now we're gonna lose it.

543
00:52:58,670 --> 00:53:00,210
- Hold everything. - Don't do anything foolish.

544
00:53:00,300 --> 00:53:02,130
I'm just gonna get the name off that freight truck.

545
00:53:02,220 --> 00:53:04,470
I'll keep an eye on the alley.

546
00:53:18,020 --> 00:53:19,650
Long distance.

547
00:53:44,800 --> 00:53:48,390
You didn't see the killing or the body. How do you know there was a murder?

548
00:53:48,470 --> 00:53:51,520
Because everything this fellow's done has been suspicious.

549
00:53:51,600 --> 00:53:55,350
Trips at night in the rain, and knives and saws and trunks with rope

550
00:53:55,440 --> 00:53:57,690
and now this wife that isn't there anymore.

551
00:53:57,770 --> 00:54:02,570
I admit it all has a mysterious sound. It could be any number of things.

552
00:54:02,650 --> 00:54:03,990
Murder's the least plausible.

553
00:54:04,070 --> 00:54:06,490
Doyle, don't tell me he's an unemployed magician,

554
00:54:06,570 --> 00:54:09,580
amusing the neighborhood with his sleight of hand. Don't tell me that.

555
00:54:09,660 --> 00:54:12,080
It's too obvious and stupid a way to commit murder.

556
00:54:12,160 --> 00:54:13,790
In full view of 50 windows?

557
00:54:13,870 --> 00:54:15,080
Then sit over there smoking a cigar,

558
00:54:15,160 --> 00:54:16,750
waiting for the police to come and pick him up?

559
00:54:16,830 --> 00:54:19,250
All right, Officer, do your duty. Go over and pick him up.

560
00:54:19,330 --> 00:54:22,590
Jeff, you've got a lot to learn about homicide.

561
00:54:22,670 --> 00:54:24,880
Why, morons have committed murder so shrewdly,

562
00:54:24,970 --> 00:54:28,220
it's taken a hundred trained police minds to catch them.

563
00:54:28,300 --> 00:54:30,930
That salesman wouldn't just knock his wife off after dinner

564
00:54:31,010 --> 00:54:33,520
and toss her in the trunk and put her in storage.

565
00:54:33,600 --> 00:54:34,930
I'll bet it's been done.

566
00:54:35,020 --> 00:54:38,230
Most everything's been done, under panic.

567
00:54:38,310 --> 00:54:40,400
This is a thousand-to-one shot.

568
00:54:40,480 --> 00:54:43,780
He's still sitting around the apartment. That man's not panicked.

569
00:54:43,860 --> 00:54:45,940
You think I made all this up?

570
00:54:46,030 --> 00:54:47,950
Well, I think you saw something.

571
00:54:48,030 --> 00:54:50,660
There's probably a very simple explanation for that.

572
00:54:50,740 --> 00:54:53,910
For instance? Wife took a trip.

573
00:54:53,990 --> 00:54:56,000
His wife was sick in bed.

574
00:54:57,460 --> 00:54:59,250
Yeah, so you told me.

575
00:55:00,830 --> 00:55:03,300
Well, Jeff, I've got to run along.

576
00:55:04,550 --> 00:55:09,800
I won't report this to the department. Let me poke into it a little on my own.

577
00:55:09,890 --> 00:55:12,640
No sense in your getting a lot of ridiculous publicity.

578
00:55:12,720 --> 00:55:14,060
Thank you.

579
00:55:16,270 --> 00:55:19,230
We know the wife is gone, so I'll see if I can find out where.

580
00:55:19,310 --> 00:55:20,560
Do that.

581
00:55:21,770 --> 00:55:25,730
You had any headaches lately? Not until you showed up.

582
00:55:26,780 --> 00:55:29,780
It'll wear off, along with the hallucinations.

583
00:55:29,860 --> 00:55:31,450
See you around.

584
00:55:55,850 --> 00:55:57,310
Get along.

585
00:56:18,580 --> 00:56:20,080
He has a six months' lease.

586
00:56:20,160 --> 00:56:23,130
Used up a little more than five and a half months of it.

587
00:56:23,210 --> 00:56:25,630
He's quiet, drinks, but not to drunkenness.

588
00:56:25,710 --> 00:56:28,630
Pays his bills promptly with money earned as a costume jewelry salesman.

589
00:56:28,710 --> 00:56:30,050
Wholesale.

590
00:56:30,300 --> 00:56:33,430
Kept to himself. None of his neighbors got close to him or his wife.

591
00:56:33,510 --> 00:56:35,350
Yeah, well, I think they missed their chance with her.

592
00:56:35,430 --> 00:56:38,260
She never left the apartment... - Where is she? The icebox? - ...until yesterday morning.

593
00:56:37,720 --> 00:56:40,060
- What time? - 6:00 a.m.

594
00:56:40,810 --> 00:56:42,140
6:00 a.m.

595
00:56:43,190 --> 00:56:45,980
I think that's about the time I fell asleep.

596
00:56:46,060 --> 00:56:47,320
Too bad.

597
00:56:47,650 --> 00:56:51,490
Thorwalds were leaving their apartment at just that time.

598
00:56:52,530 --> 00:56:54,530
Feel a little foolish?

599
00:56:54,950 --> 00:56:56,490
No, not yet.

600
00:57:08,130 --> 00:57:11,170
How's your wife? Oh, she's fine.

601
00:57:14,840 --> 00:57:16,890
Who said they left then?

602
00:57:17,510 --> 00:57:20,680
Who left where? The Thorwalds, at 6:00 in the morning.

603
00:57:20,770 --> 00:57:23,440
Oh, the building superintendent and two tenants.

604
00:57:23,520 --> 00:57:26,560
Flat statements, no hesitation. They jived with the letter.

605
00:57:26,650 --> 00:57:29,070
Thorwalds were on their way to the railroad station.

606
00:57:29,150 --> 00:57:32,690
Well, now, Tom, how could anybody possibly guess that?

607
00:57:32,780 --> 00:57:36,910
What, did they have signs on their luggage, saying, "Grand Central or bust?"

608
00:57:36,990 --> 00:57:39,740
The superintendent met Thorwald on his way back.

609
00:57:39,830 --> 00:57:42,960
Thorwald told him that he'd put his wife on a train to the country.

610
00:57:43,040 --> 00:57:46,210
I see. I'd say this is a pretty convenient guy, this superintendent.

611
00:57:46,290 --> 00:57:49,210
Have you checked his bank statements lately?

612
00:57:51,550 --> 00:57:54,090
Well, what good's his information?

613
00:57:54,170 --> 00:57:56,550
It's a secondhand version of an unsupported story

614
00:57:56,640 --> 00:57:58,390
by the murderer himself, Thorwald.

615
00:57:58,470 --> 00:58:02,020
Now did anybody actually see the wife get on the train?

616
00:58:03,020 --> 00:58:03,980
I hate to remind you,

617
00:58:04,060 --> 00:58:06,850
but this all started because you said she was murdered.

618
00:58:06,940 --> 00:58:10,070
Now, did anyone, including you, actually see her murdered?

619
00:58:10,150 --> 00:58:11,980
What are you doing?

620
00:58:12,070 --> 00:58:16,110
Are you interested in solving this case or in making me look foolish?

621
00:58:16,200 --> 00:58:18,320
Well, if possible, both.

622
00:58:18,410 --> 00:58:19,910
Well, then, do a good job of it.

623
00:58:19,990 --> 00:58:21,870
Go over there and search Thorwald's apartment.

624
00:58:21,950 --> 00:58:23,830
It must be knee-deep in evidence.

625
00:58:23,910 --> 00:58:25,370
I can't do that.

626
00:58:25,460 --> 00:58:27,710
Well, no, I don't mean right now, but when he goes out for a drink,

627
00:58:27,790 --> 00:58:30,040
or a paper, or something. What he doesn't know won't hurt him.

628
00:58:30,130 --> 00:58:32,500
I can't do that, even if he isn't there.

629
00:58:32,590 --> 00:58:37,340
What's he... Does he have a courtesy card from the police department, or something?

630
00:58:37,430 --> 00:58:39,260
Now don't get me mad.

631
00:58:39,470 --> 00:58:42,600
Not even a detective can walk into an apartment and search it.

632
00:58:42,680 --> 00:58:45,770
If I were caught in there, they'd have my badge within 10 minutes.

633
00:58:45,850 --> 00:58:48,350
All right. Make sure you don't get caught, that's all.

634
00:58:48,440 --> 00:58:50,690
What? If you find something, you've got a murderer,

635
00:58:50,770 --> 00:58:52,860
and they don't care anything about a couple of house rules.

636
00:58:52,940 --> 00:58:55,030
If you don't find anything, the fellow's clear.

637
00:58:55,110 --> 00:58:59,530
At the risk of sounding stuffy, I'd like to remind you of the Constitution

638
00:58:59,610 --> 00:59:02,490
and the phrase, "Search warrant issued by a judge

639
00:59:02,580 --> 00:59:04,620
"who knows his Bill of Rights verbatim."

640
00:59:04,700 --> 00:59:07,750
He must ask for evidence. Give him evidence.

641
00:59:09,580 --> 00:59:11,500
Yeah, I can hear myself.

642
00:59:11,590 --> 00:59:15,630
"Your Honor, I have a friend who's an amateur sleuth.

643
00:59:16,630 --> 00:59:20,390
"Well, the other night after having a heavy dinner, he..."

644
00:59:20,470 --> 00:59:22,930
He'd throw the New York State penal code right in my face.

645
00:59:23,010 --> 00:59:25,060
And there's six volumes.

646
00:59:25,810 --> 00:59:28,520
You know, by tomorrow morning there may not be any evidence left

647
00:59:28,600 --> 00:59:30,730
over in that apartment. You know that.

648
00:59:30,810 --> 00:59:33,110
It's a detective's nightmare.

649
00:59:33,520 --> 00:59:35,900
What do you need before you can search?

650
00:59:35,980 --> 00:59:37,190
Tell me. Now, what do you need?

651
00:59:37,280 --> 00:59:39,780
Bloody footsteps leading up to the door?

652
00:59:39,860 --> 00:59:43,530
One thing I don't need is heckling. You called me and asked for help.

653
00:59:43,620 --> 00:59:46,160
Now you're behaving like a taxpayer.

654
00:59:47,000 --> 00:59:48,910
How did we ever stand each other in that plane

655
00:59:49,000 --> 00:59:51,290
for three years during the war?

656
00:59:53,340 --> 00:59:56,300
I guess I'll go over to the railroad station and check on Thorwald's story.

657
00:59:56,380 --> 00:59:58,090
Oh, forget about the story.

658
00:59:58,170 --> 01:00:00,840
Find the trunk. Mrs. Thorwald's in it.

659
01:00:01,220 --> 01:00:02,430
Oh, I almost forgot.

660
01:00:02,510 --> 01:00:05,890
There was a postcard in Thorwald's mailbox. It was

661
01:00:06,260 --> 01:00:09,140
mailed 3:30 yesterday afternoon from Merritsville.

662
01:00:09,230 --> 01:00:11,310
That's 80 miles north of here.

663
01:00:11,390 --> 01:00:15,190
Message went, "Arrived okay. Already feeling better.

664
01:00:15,270 --> 01:00:16,730
"Love, Anna."

665
01:00:20,650 --> 01:00:22,030
Is that...

666
01:00:22,990 --> 01:00:25,490
Anna... Is that who I think it is?

667
01:00:26,700 --> 01:00:28,200
Mrs. Thorwald.

668
01:00:31,670 --> 01:00:34,000
Oh, so anything you need, Jeff?

669
01:00:34,380 --> 01:00:36,920
You might send me a good detective.

670
01:04:24,310 --> 01:04:25,980
- Hello? - Hello, Miss Doyle?

671
01:04:26,070 --> 01:04:28,690
Yes? This is Jeff again. Has Tom come in yet?

672
01:04:28,780 --> 01:04:29,740
Not yet, Jeff.

673
01:04:29,820 --> 01:04:31,820
You mean you haven't even heard from him?

674
01:04:31,910 --> 01:04:35,410
Not a word. Is it something really important, Jeff?

675
01:04:35,490 --> 01:04:37,160
Yeah, I'm afraid it is, Tess.

676
01:04:37,240 --> 01:04:39,250
I'll have him call the moment I hear from him.

677
01:04:39,330 --> 01:04:40,710
No, no, don't bother to do that.

678
01:04:40,790 --> 01:04:43,380
Just have him get over here as soon as he can.

679
01:04:43,460 --> 01:04:45,170
Looks like Thorwald's pulling out tonight.

680
01:04:45,250 --> 01:04:47,420
Who's Thorwald? He'll know, he'll know.

681
01:04:47,500 --> 01:04:50,220
And Thorwald's a man, Tess, don't worry.

682
01:04:50,300 --> 01:04:52,880
- Good night, you idiot. - Good night.

683
01:05:09,440 --> 01:05:11,280
Long distance again.

684
01:05:46,860 --> 01:05:49,150
There's somebody at the door.

685
01:05:53,360 --> 01:05:54,780
- Hi. - Hello.

686
01:06:17,720 --> 01:06:19,050
Hello.

687
01:06:22,390 --> 01:06:23,640
What'd you do to your hair?

688
01:06:23,730 --> 01:06:25,480
- Oh, well, I just sort of... - Take a look at Thorwald.

689
01:06:25,560 --> 01:06:29,020
He's getting ready to pull out for good. Look at him.

690
01:06:32,230 --> 01:06:33,990
He doesn't seem to be in any hurry.

691
01:06:34,070 --> 01:06:36,570
He's been laying out all his things on one of the beds.

692
01:06:36,650 --> 01:06:38,910
Shirts, suits, coats, socks.

693
01:06:38,990 --> 01:06:41,160
Even his wife's... That alligator handbag

694
01:06:41,240 --> 01:06:43,540
- his wife had on the bedpost. - What about it?

695
01:06:43,620 --> 01:06:46,370
He had it hidden in the dresser. At least, it was there.

696
01:06:46,460 --> 01:06:50,380
He took it out, went to the telephone and made a long-distance call.

697
01:06:50,460 --> 01:06:53,170
He had his wife's jewelry in the handbag.

698
01:06:53,250 --> 01:06:54,300
Seemed worried about it.

699
01:06:54,380 --> 01:06:56,170
Asked somebody's advice over the telephone.

700
01:06:56,260 --> 01:06:57,880
Someone not his wife.

701
01:06:57,970 --> 01:07:00,140
Well, I never saw him ask her for advice.

702
01:07:00,220 --> 01:07:04,060
She volunteered plenty, but I never saw him ask her for any.

703
01:07:14,440 --> 01:07:17,240
I wonder where he's going. I don't know.

704
01:07:18,070 --> 01:07:22,410
Suppose he doesn't come back again? He'll be back. His things are still there.

705
01:07:23,620 --> 01:07:25,540
Well, I guess it's safe to put on some lights now.

706
01:07:25,620 --> 01:07:27,120
No, not yet!

707
01:07:34,130 --> 01:07:38,550
Okay, you can turn it on now. He must have gone someplace to the right.

708
01:07:39,680 --> 01:07:43,010
All day long, I've been trying to keep my mind on my work.

709
01:07:43,100 --> 01:07:46,980
Thinking about Thorwald? And you and your friend, Doyle.

710
01:07:47,480 --> 01:07:50,560
Did you hear from him again, since he left? Not a word.

711
01:07:50,650 --> 01:07:53,230
He said he was gonna check the railroad station and the trunk.

712
01:07:53,310 --> 01:07:55,190
He must be still at it.

713
01:07:55,860 --> 01:07:57,820
Something on your mind?

714
01:07:58,530 --> 01:08:01,610
It doesn't make sense to me. What doesn't?

715
01:08:02,070 --> 01:08:04,490
Women aren't that unpredictable.

716
01:08:06,080 --> 01:08:09,210
Well, I can't guess what you're thinking.

717
01:08:09,290 --> 01:08:11,670
A woman has a favorite handbag

718
01:08:11,750 --> 01:08:15,880
and it always hangs on her bedpost, where she can get at it easily.

719
01:08:15,960 --> 01:08:19,970
And then, all of a sudden, she goes away on a trip and leaves it behind. Why?

720
01:08:20,050 --> 01:08:21,720
Because she didn't know she was going on a trip.

721
01:08:21,800 --> 01:08:23,800
And where she's going, she wouldn't need the handbag.

722
01:08:23,890 --> 01:08:26,760
Yes, but only her husband would know that.

723
01:08:28,020 --> 01:08:29,270
And that jewelry.

724
01:08:29,350 --> 01:08:31,140
Women don't keep their jewelry in a purse

725
01:08:31,230 --> 01:08:33,730
getting all twisted and scratched and tangled up.

726
01:08:33,810 --> 01:08:35,400
Well, do they hide it in their husband's clothes?

727
01:08:35,480 --> 01:08:37,070
They do not.

728
01:08:37,150 --> 01:08:39,860
And they don't leave it behind, either.

729
01:08:40,280 --> 01:08:42,320
Why, a woman going anywhere but the hospital

730
01:08:42,410 --> 01:08:45,410
would always take makeup, perfume and jewelry.

731
01:08:46,990 --> 01:08:48,870
Put that over there. That's inside stuff, huh?

732
01:08:48,950 --> 01:08:50,580
It's basic equipment.

733
01:08:50,660 --> 01:08:53,540
And you don't leave it behind in your husband's drawer

734
01:08:53,620 --> 01:08:55,080
in your favorite handbag.

735
01:08:55,170 --> 01:08:57,460
Well, I'm with you, sweetie. I'm with you.

736
01:08:57,550 --> 01:08:59,510
Tom Doyle has a pat answer for that, though.

737
01:08:59,590 --> 01:09:01,550
That Mrs. Thorwald left at 6:00 a.m.

738
01:09:01,630 --> 01:09:04,470
yesterday with her husband? According to those witnesses.

739
01:09:04,550 --> 01:09:06,800
Well, I have a pat rebuttal for Mr. Doyle.

740
01:09:06,890 --> 01:09:10,730
That couldn't have been Mrs. Thorwald, or I don't know women.

741
01:09:10,810 --> 01:09:12,850
What about the witnesses?

742
01:09:12,940 --> 01:09:15,190
We'll agree they saw a woman,

743
01:09:15,730 --> 01:09:17,980
but she was not Mrs. Thorwald.

744
01:09:18,360 --> 01:09:21,740
That is, not yet. Is that so?

745
01:09:25,910 --> 01:09:27,330
Come here.

746
01:09:30,750 --> 01:09:34,170
I'd like to see your friend's face when we tell him.

747
01:09:34,250 --> 01:09:36,460
He doesn't sound like much of a detective.

748
01:09:36,540 --> 01:09:40,000
Oh, don't be too hard on him. He's a steady worker.

749
01:09:41,460 --> 01:09:43,590
I sure wish he'd show up.

750
01:09:43,670 --> 01:09:46,260
Don't rush him. We have all night.

751
01:09:48,850 --> 01:09:50,890
- We have all what? - Night.

752
01:09:51,430 --> 01:09:53,600
I'm going to stay with you.

753
01:09:55,560 --> 01:09:58,820
Well, you'll have to clear that with my landlord.

754
01:09:59,730 --> 01:10:01,940
I have the whole weekend off.

755
01:10:02,900 --> 01:10:05,820
That's very nice, but I just have one bed.

756
01:10:06,530 --> 01:10:10,160
If you say anything else, I'll stay tomorrow night, too.

757
01:10:11,080 --> 01:10:14,120
I won't be able to give you any pajamas.

758
01:10:19,540 --> 01:10:22,670
You said I'll have to live out of one suitcase.

759
01:10:25,050 --> 01:10:28,800
I'll bet yours isn't this small. This is a suitcase?

760
01:10:28,890 --> 01:10:31,810
Well, a Mark Cross overnight case, anyway.

761
01:10:32,270 --> 01:10:35,390
Compact, but ample enough.

762
01:10:35,480 --> 01:10:37,730
Looks like you packed in a hurry.

763
01:10:37,810 --> 01:10:40,310
Look at this. Isn't that amazing?

764
01:10:41,150 --> 01:10:42,780
I'll trade you...

765
01:10:42,860 --> 01:10:45,950
My feminine intuition for a bed for the night.

766
01:10:46,030 --> 01:10:48,110
I'll go along with that.

767
01:11:01,920 --> 01:11:04,050
There's that song again.

768
01:11:10,680 --> 01:11:14,350
Where does a man get inspiration to write a song like that?

769
01:11:14,430 --> 01:11:17,520
Well, he gets it from the landlady once a month.

770
01:11:20,610 --> 01:11:22,570
It's utterly beautiful.

771
01:11:23,400 --> 01:11:26,860
I wish I could be creative. Oh, sweetie, you are.

772
01:11:27,780 --> 01:11:31,280
You have a great talent for creating difficult situations.

773
01:11:31,370 --> 01:11:32,660
- I do? - Sure.

774
01:11:32,740 --> 01:11:35,120
Like staying here all night uninvited.

775
01:11:35,200 --> 01:11:38,540
Surprise is the most important element of attack.

776
01:11:38,620 --> 01:11:41,750
And besides, you're not up on your private eye literature.

777
01:11:41,830 --> 01:11:43,960
When they're in trouble, it's always their Girl Friday

778
01:11:44,050 --> 01:11:45,380
who gets them out of it.

779
01:11:45,460 --> 01:11:47,630
Is she the girl that saves them from the clutches

780
01:11:47,720 --> 01:11:49,180
of the seductive showgirls

781
01:11:49,260 --> 01:11:51,470
and the over-passionate daughters of the rich?

782
01:11:51,550 --> 01:11:53,640
- The same. - That's the one, huh?

783
01:11:53,720 --> 01:11:57,480
It's funny. He never ends up marrying her, does he?

784
01:11:57,560 --> 01:11:59,940
That's strange. Weird.

785
01:12:03,060 --> 01:12:05,270
Why don't I slip into something more comfortable?

786
01:12:05,360 --> 01:12:07,070
By all means.

787
01:12:07,150 --> 01:12:09,990
I mean, like the kitchen and make us some coffee.

788
01:12:10,070 --> 01:12:12,570
Oh, and some brandy, too, huh?

789
01:12:22,330 --> 01:12:23,670
Harry.

790
01:12:33,090 --> 01:12:34,470
- Jeff. - Hi.

791
01:13:26,940 --> 01:13:28,730
What else have you got on this man Thorwald?

792
01:13:28,820 --> 01:13:32,240
Well, enough to scare me you wouldn't show up in time and we'd lose him.

793
01:13:32,320 --> 01:13:33,280
Think he's getting out of here?

794
01:13:33,360 --> 01:13:34,820
Look, he's got everything he owns

795
01:13:34,910 --> 01:13:38,490
laid out over there in the bedroom, waiting to be packed.

796
01:13:40,370 --> 01:13:44,000
I'm just warming some brandy. Mr. Doyle, I presume?

797
01:13:46,630 --> 01:13:49,880
Tom, this is Miss Lisa Fremont. How do you do?

798
01:13:50,670 --> 01:13:52,880
We think Thorwald's guilty.

799
01:13:59,850 --> 01:14:01,520
Careful, Tom.

800
01:14:06,980 --> 01:14:08,020
- Hello. - Lieutenant Doyle.

801
01:14:08,110 --> 01:14:10,480
Yeah, he's right here. For you.

802
01:14:11,360 --> 01:14:13,400
- Hello. - Lieutenant Doyle, sir?

803
01:14:13,490 --> 01:14:14,780
Speaking.

804
01:14:15,820 --> 01:14:17,160
Yeah.

805
01:14:19,200 --> 01:14:20,530
All right.

806
01:14:20,870 --> 01:14:23,120
I see. Thank you. Goodbye.

807
01:14:23,620 --> 01:14:25,790
Coffee will be ready soon.

808
01:14:25,870 --> 01:14:28,630
Jeff, aren't you going to tell him about the jewelry?

809
01:14:28,710 --> 01:14:29,750
Jewelry?

810
01:14:29,840 --> 01:14:33,210
He's got his wife's jewelry hidden in his clothes in the bedroom over there.

811
01:14:33,300 --> 01:14:36,550
You sure it belonged to his wife? It was in her favorite handbag.

812
01:14:36,630 --> 01:14:39,260
Mr. Doyle, that can only lead to one conclusion.

813
01:14:39,350 --> 01:14:40,390
Namely?

814
01:14:40,470 --> 01:14:44,480
That it was not Mrs. Thorwald that left with him yesterday morning.

815
01:14:44,560 --> 01:14:45,980
You figured that out, eh?

816
01:14:46,060 --> 01:14:48,480
Well, it's simply that women don't leave their jewelry behind

817
01:14:48,560 --> 01:14:50,560
when they go on a trip.

818
01:14:50,860 --> 01:14:51,860
Come on, come on, Tom.

819
01:14:51,940 --> 01:14:55,570
Now you don't really need any of this information, do you?

820
01:15:01,450 --> 01:15:03,740
As a matter of fact, I don't.

821
01:15:09,580 --> 01:15:12,920
Lars Thorwald is no more a murderer than I am.

822
01:15:16,880 --> 01:15:19,510
You mean to say you can explain everything that's gone on over there,

823
01:15:19,590 --> 01:15:20,890
and is still going on?

824
01:15:20,970 --> 01:15:22,850
No, neither can you.

825
01:15:22,930 --> 01:15:25,930
That's a secret, private world you're looking into out there.

826
01:15:26,020 --> 01:15:28,980
People do a lot of things in private they couldn't possibly explain in public.

827
01:15:29,060 --> 01:15:30,940
Like disposing of their wives?

828
01:15:31,020 --> 01:15:33,770
You get that idea out of your mind. It'll only lead in the wrong direction.

829
01:15:33,860 --> 01:15:36,320
What about the knife and the saw?

830
01:15:36,400 --> 01:15:37,990
Did you ever own a saw?

831
01:15:38,070 --> 01:15:40,740
Well, at home in the garage I had...

832
01:15:40,820 --> 01:15:43,120
How many people did you cut up with it?

833
01:15:43,200 --> 01:15:47,620
Or with the couple of hundred knives you've probably owned in your lifetime?

834
01:15:48,370 --> 01:15:50,040
Well, your logic is backward.

835
01:15:50,120 --> 01:15:54,000
You can't ignore the wife disappearing and the trunk and the jewelry.

836
01:15:54,090 --> 01:15:56,840
I checked the railroad station. He bought a ticket.

837
01:15:56,920 --> 01:15:58,880
Ten minutes later, he put his wife on a train.

838
01:15:58,970 --> 01:16:02,090
Destination, Merritsville. The witnesses are that deep.

839
01:16:02,180 --> 01:16:05,220
That might have been a woman, but it couldn't have been Mrs. Thorwald.

840
01:16:05,310 --> 01:16:06,850
- That jewelry... - Look, Miss Fremont.

841
01:16:06,930 --> 01:16:09,730
That feminine intuition stuff sells magazines.

842
01:16:09,810 --> 01:16:12,770
But in real life, it's still a fairy tale.

843
01:16:12,860 --> 01:16:14,570
I don't know how many wasted years I've spent

844
01:16:14,650 --> 01:16:17,230
tracking down leads based on female intuition.

845
01:16:17,320 --> 01:16:19,950
All right. I take it you didn't find the trunk,

846
01:16:20,030 --> 01:16:23,530
and all this is from an old speech you once made at the Policemen's Ball.

847
01:16:23,620 --> 01:16:26,120
I found the trunk a half-hour after I left here.

848
01:16:26,200 --> 01:16:29,870
I suppose it's normal for a man to tie up a trunk with heavy rope?

849
01:16:29,960 --> 01:16:31,960
Well, if the lock is broken, yes.

850
01:16:32,040 --> 01:16:35,170
And what did you find inside the trunk? A surly note to me?

851
01:16:35,250 --> 01:16:37,340
Mrs. Thorwald's clothes.

852
01:16:37,420 --> 01:16:40,840
Clean, well packed. Not stylish, but presentable.

853
01:16:40,930 --> 01:16:42,760
Didn't you take them off to the crime lab?

854
01:16:42,840 --> 01:16:45,720
I sent them on their merry and legal way.

855
01:16:45,810 --> 01:16:48,600
Why, when a woman is taking a simple, short trip,

856
01:16:48,680 --> 01:16:50,600
does she take everything she owns?

857
01:16:50,680 --> 01:16:54,400
Let's let the female psychology department handle that one.

858
01:16:55,360 --> 01:16:57,820
I would say it looked as if she wasn't coming back.

859
01:16:57,900 --> 01:16:59,610
That's what's known as a family problem.

860
01:16:59,690 --> 01:17:02,530
And if she wasn't coming back, why didn't he tell his landlord?

861
01:17:02,610 --> 01:17:07,410
I'll tell you why he didn't tell his landlord. Because he was hiding something.

862
01:17:09,500 --> 01:17:12,000
Do you tell your landlord everything?

863
01:17:12,080 --> 01:17:13,790
I told you to be careful, Tom.

864
01:17:13,870 --> 01:17:17,040
Look, if I'd have been careful piloting that reconnaissance plane during the war,

865
01:17:17,130 --> 01:17:18,630
you wouldn't have had the chance to take the pictures

866
01:17:18,710 --> 01:17:23,130
that won you a medal, and a good job, and fame and money.

867
01:17:25,430 --> 01:17:30,020
What do you say we all sit down and have a nice, friendly drink, too? Hmm?

868
01:17:30,810 --> 01:17:32,730
Forget all about this.

869
01:17:33,390 --> 01:17:36,980
We can tell lies about the good old days during the war.

870
01:17:37,940 --> 01:17:40,030
You mean you're through with the case?

871
01:17:40,110 --> 01:17:42,950
There is no case to be through with, Miss Fremont.

872
01:17:43,030 --> 01:17:44,910
How about that drink?

873
01:17:52,500 --> 01:17:54,620
Yeah, I guess you're right.

874
01:17:55,880 --> 01:17:59,250
Well, I think I better get home and get some sleep.

875
01:18:00,840 --> 01:18:02,260
Cheers.

876
01:18:06,720 --> 01:18:08,800
Not much of a snifter.

877
01:18:13,560 --> 01:18:16,190
Oh, Jeff, if you need any more help...

878
01:18:16,270 --> 01:18:19,770
Consult the Yellow Pages in your telephone directory.

879
01:18:20,440 --> 01:18:23,110
Oh, I love funny exiting lines.

880
01:18:24,190 --> 01:18:27,410
Who was that trunk addressed to? Mrs. Anna Thorwald.

881
01:18:27,490 --> 01:18:30,030
Well, then let's wait and find out who picks it up.

882
01:18:30,120 --> 01:18:32,540
Oh, that telephone call. I gave them your number.

883
01:18:32,620 --> 01:18:35,540
I hope you don't mind. Depends on who they were.

884
01:18:35,620 --> 01:18:37,210
The police at Merritsville.

885
01:18:37,290 --> 01:18:39,460
They reported the trunk was just picked up

886
01:18:39,540 --> 01:18:41,500
by Mrs. Anna Thorwald.

887
01:18:42,420 --> 01:18:44,840
Don't stay up too late.

888
01:19:03,150 --> 01:19:05,070
- Look. - What?

889
01:19:37,060 --> 01:19:39,650
Yeah, he's kind of young, isn't he?

890
01:20:15,510 --> 01:20:17,270
What are you doing?

891
01:20:43,580 --> 01:20:48,210
You know, much as I hate to give Thomas J. Doyle too much credit,

892
01:20:48,300 --> 01:20:50,590
he might have gotten a hold of something when he said

893
01:20:50,670 --> 01:20:53,840
that was pretty private stuff going on out there.

894
01:20:55,600 --> 01:21:00,770
I wonder if it's ethical to watch a man with binoculars and a long-focus lens.

895
01:21:02,600 --> 01:21:05,520
Do you... Do you suppose it's ethical,

896
01:21:05,610 --> 01:21:08,780
even if you prove that he didn't commit a crime?

897
01:21:10,360 --> 01:21:13,030
I'm not much on rear-window ethics.

898
01:21:13,110 --> 01:21:14,700
Of course, they can do the same thing to me.

899
01:21:14,780 --> 01:21:18,290
Watch me like a bug under a glass, if they want to.

900
01:21:18,370 --> 01:21:21,870
Jeff, you know, if someone came in here, they wouldn't believe what they'd see.

901
01:21:21,960 --> 01:21:24,250
What? You and me with long faces.

902
01:21:24,330 --> 01:21:28,840
Plunged into despair because we find out a man didn't kill his wife.

903
01:21:28,920 --> 01:21:32,380
We're two of the most frightening ghouls I've ever known.

904
01:21:32,470 --> 01:21:37,430
You'd think we could be a little bit happy that the poor woman is alive and well.

905
01:21:39,970 --> 01:21:43,640
Whatever happened to that old saying, "Love thy neighbor"?

906
01:21:44,980 --> 01:21:48,360
You know, I think I'll start reviving that tomorrow.

907
01:21:49,570 --> 01:21:51,990
Yeah, I'll begin with Miss Torso.

908
01:21:52,820 --> 01:21:56,700
Not if I have to move into an apartment across the way

909
01:21:58,330 --> 01:22:01,500
and do the Dance of the Seven Veils every hour.

910
01:22:04,460 --> 01:22:06,460
Show's over for tonight.

911
01:22:11,590 --> 01:22:13,920
Preview of coming attractions.

912
01:22:16,340 --> 01:22:19,050
Did Mr. Doyle think I stole this case?

913
01:22:20,510 --> 01:22:23,390
No, Lisa, I don't think he did.

914
01:22:38,410 --> 01:22:41,450
What do you think? Well...

915
01:22:42,660 --> 01:22:46,080
I will rephrase the question. Thank you.

916
01:22:47,170 --> 01:22:48,750
Do you like it?

917
01:22:49,540 --> 01:22:53,960
Yes, I like it. Well...

918
01:23:02,600 --> 01:23:04,350
What's the matter?

919
01:23:07,060 --> 01:23:09,100
- Somebody's hurt? - It's the dog.

920
01:23:09,190 --> 01:23:11,690
Something's happened to the dog.

921
01:23:14,440 --> 01:23:17,650
What's the trouble? Somebody fall out a window?

922
01:23:18,320 --> 01:23:20,620
See, it's a dog down there.

923
01:23:21,580 --> 01:23:22,950
It's dead.

924
01:23:23,540 --> 01:23:26,370
It's been strangled. The neck is broken.

925
01:23:27,210 --> 01:23:29,000
Which one of you did it?

926
01:23:29,080 --> 01:23:31,500
Which one of you killed my dog?

927
01:23:33,210 --> 01:23:36,010
You don't know the meaning of the word "Neighbors."

928
01:23:36,510 --> 01:23:40,050
Neighbors like each other, speak to each other,

929
01:23:40,140 --> 01:23:42,470
care if anybody lives or dies.

930
01:23:43,180 --> 01:23:44,970
But none of you do!

931
01:23:48,890 --> 01:23:51,360
But I couldn't imagine any of you being so low

932
01:23:51,440 --> 01:23:54,820
that you'd kill a little helpless, friendly dog.

933
01:23:56,990 --> 01:24:00,860
The only thing in this whole neighborhood who liked anybody.

934
01:24:09,160 --> 01:24:11,920
Did you kill him because he liked you?

935
01:24:12,000 --> 01:24:14,170
Just because he liked you?

936
01:24:16,960 --> 01:24:18,630
Let's go inside.

937
01:24:22,800 --> 01:24:25,720
Come on. Let's go back in. It's only a dog.

938
01:24:38,070 --> 01:24:42,070
You know, for a minute, that Tom Doyle almost had me convinced I was wrong.

939
01:24:42,160 --> 01:24:44,070
- But you're not? - Look.

940
01:24:44,160 --> 01:24:48,370
In the whole courtyard, only one person didn't come to the window.

941
01:24:48,450 --> 01:24:49,660
Look.

942
01:24:55,040 --> 01:24:58,420
Why would Thorwald want to kill a little dog?

943
01:25:00,300 --> 01:25:02,300
Because it knew too much?

944
01:25:10,350 --> 01:25:13,400
You think this is worth waiting all day to see?

945
01:25:13,900 --> 01:25:15,440
Is he cleaning house?

946
01:25:15,520 --> 01:25:19,230
He's washing down the bathroom walls. Hmm.

947
01:25:19,320 --> 01:25:21,450
Must have splattered a lot.

948
01:25:22,990 --> 01:25:25,700
Well, why not? That's what we're all thinking.

949
01:25:25,780 --> 01:25:28,620
He killed her in there, he has to clean up those stains before he leaves.

950
01:25:28,700 --> 01:25:31,370
Oh, Stella, your choice of words.

951
01:25:31,460 --> 01:25:34,880
Nobody ever invented a polite word for a killing yet.

952
01:25:37,960 --> 01:25:42,970
Lisa? Lisa, back there, on that shelf, there's a little yellow box. You see it?

953
01:25:43,050 --> 01:25:44,800
- Top one? - Right on top, yeah.

954
01:25:44,890 --> 01:25:47,300
Bring it. And bring me the viewer there.

955
01:25:49,260 --> 01:25:50,850
I just got a...

956
01:25:51,560 --> 01:25:54,310
Now, these are about two weeks old.

957
01:25:54,390 --> 01:25:58,070
I hope I took something else besides leg art. Now which one...

958
01:25:58,150 --> 01:26:00,530
- What are you looking for? - Here.

959
01:26:00,980 --> 01:26:04,200
There's something, and if I'm right, I think I've solved a murder.

960
01:26:04,280 --> 01:26:06,070
- Mrs. Thorwald? - No.

961
01:26:07,240 --> 01:26:09,280
No, the dog.

962
01:26:11,700 --> 01:26:14,710
I think I know why Thorwald killed that dog.

963
01:26:15,250 --> 01:26:18,380
Here, now you take a look. Tell me what you see.

964
01:26:18,710 --> 01:26:20,340
Now take it down.

965
01:26:20,840 --> 01:26:24,470
Now look again. Now take it down. You see?

966
01:26:25,590 --> 01:26:28,140
But it's just a picture of the backyard, that's all.

967
01:26:28,220 --> 01:26:32,220
One important change. One important change.

968
01:26:33,060 --> 01:26:36,270
Those flowers in Thorwald's pet flower bed.

969
01:26:37,190 --> 01:26:39,270
You mean where the dog was sniffing around?

970
01:26:39,360 --> 01:26:42,820
Where the dog was digging. Now look at those flowers.

971
01:26:43,240 --> 01:26:46,990
Look, right? Those two yellow zinnias on this end

972
01:26:47,070 --> 01:26:48,910
aren't as tall as they were.

973
01:26:48,990 --> 01:26:52,330
Now, since when do flowers grow shorter in two weeks?

974
01:26:52,410 --> 01:26:55,660
There's something buried there. Mrs. Thorwald.

975
01:26:56,290 --> 01:26:59,540
You haven't spent much time around cemeteries, have you?

976
01:26:59,630 --> 01:27:02,670
Mr. Thorwald could scarcely put his wife's body

977
01:27:02,750 --> 01:27:05,720
in a plot of ground about one foot square.

978
01:27:05,800 --> 01:27:08,640
Unless, of course, he put her in standing on end,

979
01:27:08,720 --> 01:27:11,430
and then he wouldn't need a knife and saw.

980
01:27:11,510 --> 01:27:15,180
No, my idea is she's scattered all over town.

981
01:27:15,270 --> 01:27:17,560
- Leg in the East River... - Oh, Stella, please!

982
01:27:17,640 --> 01:27:19,060
No, no, no. There's something in there.

983
01:27:19,150 --> 01:27:21,730
Those flowers have been taken out and put back in.

984
01:27:21,820 --> 01:27:23,690
- Maybe it's the knife and saw. - Yeah.

985
01:27:23,780 --> 01:27:26,070
Call Lieutenant Doyle. No, let's wait.

986
01:27:26,150 --> 01:27:29,910
Let's wait till it gets a little darker, and I'll go over there and dig them up.

987
01:27:29,990 --> 01:27:33,990
You'll go? You won't dig anything up and get your neck broken.

988
01:27:34,620 --> 01:27:36,290
No, no, we've...

989
01:27:36,750 --> 01:27:40,130
We're not gonna call Doyle until I can produce Mrs. Thorwald's body.

990
01:27:40,210 --> 01:27:43,040
What we've got to do is find a way to get into that apartment.

991
01:27:43,130 --> 01:27:44,590
He's packing.

992
01:27:46,970 --> 01:27:49,220
Here, get me a pencil.

993
01:27:50,260 --> 01:27:53,560
Stella, get me some note paper. It's up here someplace.

994
01:27:53,640 --> 01:27:55,180
There it is.

995
01:29:11,840 --> 01:29:14,680
You did it, Thorwald. You did it.

996
01:29:18,720 --> 01:29:21,980
Look out, Lisa. He's coming. You never should have let her do it. If he ever...

997
01:29:20,980 --> 01:29:23,350
Look. Look. Look.

998
01:29:43,290 --> 01:29:45,710
Well, thank heaven that's over.

999
01:29:48,960 --> 01:29:52,130
All right if I have a drink? Sure, go ahead.

1000
01:30:00,140 --> 01:30:04,060
There's no doubt about it. He's leaving. It's just a question of when.

1001
01:30:04,140 --> 01:30:06,810
Mind if I use that portable keyhole?

1002
01:30:08,110 --> 01:30:11,990
Go ahead, just as long as you tell me what you're looking at.

1003
01:30:17,990 --> 01:30:20,200
I wonder... Wonder what?

1004
01:30:20,290 --> 01:30:22,160
Miss Lonelyheart's just laid out something

1005
01:30:22,250 --> 01:30:24,870
that looks like rhodium tri-eckonal capsules.

1006
01:30:24,960 --> 01:30:26,250
You can tell from here?

1007
01:30:26,330 --> 01:30:29,290
I've handled enough of those red pills to put everybody in Hackensack

1008
01:30:29,380 --> 01:30:30,670
to sleep for the winter.

1009
01:30:30,750 --> 01:30:32,420
Well, does she have enough of them to...

1010
01:30:32,510 --> 01:30:34,510
No. It will make the rest easy to...

1011
01:30:35,260 --> 01:30:37,010
Wasn't that close?

1012
01:30:37,640 --> 01:30:40,760
What was his reaction. I mean, when he looked at the note?

1013
01:30:40,850 --> 01:30:44,770
Well, it wasn't the kind of an expression that would get him a quick loan at the bank.

1014
01:30:44,850 --> 01:30:46,690
Jeff, the handbag!

1015
01:31:12,000 --> 01:31:14,340
Suppose Mrs. Thorwald's wedding ring

1016
01:31:14,420 --> 01:31:17,430
is among the jewelry he has in that handbag?

1017
01:31:17,510 --> 01:31:20,100
Now, during the phone conversation he held up three rings.

1018
01:31:20,180 --> 01:31:23,520
One with a diamond, one with a big stone of some sort

1019
01:31:23,600 --> 01:31:25,480
and then just a plain gold band.

1020
01:31:25,560 --> 01:31:29,650
And the last thing she would leave behind would be a wedding ring.

1021
01:31:29,730 --> 01:31:31,940
Stella, do you ever leave yours at home?

1022
01:31:32,020 --> 01:31:36,490
The only way anybody could get that ring would be to chop off my finger.

1023
01:31:39,490 --> 01:31:42,660
Let's go down and find out what's buried in the garden.

1024
01:31:42,740 --> 01:31:47,160
Why not? I've always wanted to meet Mrs. Thorwald.

1025
01:31:47,250 --> 01:31:48,830
- What are you two talking about? - You got a shovel?

1026
01:31:48,920 --> 01:31:52,040
A shovel? Of course I don't have a shovel. There's probably one in the basement.

1027
01:31:52,130 --> 01:31:54,170
Jeff, if you're squeamish, just don't look.

1028
01:31:54,250 --> 01:31:55,550
Squeamish? I'm not squeamish.

1029
01:31:55,630 --> 01:31:59,180
I just don't want you two to end up like that dog ended up. That's all.

1030
01:31:59,260 --> 01:32:02,510
Oh, you know, Miss Fremont, he might just have something there.

1031
01:32:02,600 --> 01:32:04,680
Just hold on. Here, take this.

1032
01:32:04,760 --> 01:32:07,100
No sense taking any chances in this thing.

1033
01:32:07,180 --> 01:32:09,440
Here, give me the phone book.

1034
01:32:10,600 --> 01:32:11,860
What for?

1035
01:32:12,610 --> 01:32:14,570
Maybe I can get him out of that apartment.

1036
01:32:14,650 --> 01:32:17,940
We only need a few minutes. I'll see if I can get about 15 minutes.

1037
01:32:18,030 --> 01:32:20,160
How? Thorwald.

1038
01:32:21,780 --> 01:32:24,620
Thorwald... Thorwald...

1039
01:32:26,080 --> 01:32:29,410
Chelsea 27099.

1040
01:32:31,960 --> 01:32:35,130
We scared him once, maybe we can scare him again.

1041
01:32:35,880 --> 01:32:38,880
Well, I guess I'm using that word "we" a little freely.

1042
01:32:38,970 --> 01:32:42,930
I mean, you're taking all the chances. Shall we vote him in, Stella?

1043
01:32:43,010 --> 01:32:44,050
Unanimously.

1044
01:32:44,140 --> 01:32:46,430
27099. Look out.

1045
01:32:49,730 --> 01:32:51,020
Chelsea.

1046
01:32:53,060 --> 01:32:54,400
Chelsea.

1047
01:33:12,370 --> 01:33:14,580
Go on, pick it up, Thorwald.

1048
01:33:15,590 --> 01:33:17,460
Go on, you're curious.

1049
01:33:18,340 --> 01:33:21,590
You wonder if it's your girlfriend calling, the one you killed for.

1050
01:33:21,670 --> 01:33:23,340
Go on, pick it up.

1051
01:33:27,640 --> 01:33:28,810
Hello?

1052
01:33:29,770 --> 01:33:31,640
Did you get my note?

1053
01:33:33,690 --> 01:33:35,900
Well, did you get it, Thorwald?

1054
01:33:35,980 --> 01:33:37,150
Who are you?

1055
01:33:37,230 --> 01:33:39,440
I'll give you a chance to find out.

1056
01:33:39,530 --> 01:33:42,280
Meet me in the bar at the Albert Hotel.

1057
01:33:42,360 --> 01:33:44,110
Do it right away.

1058
01:33:44,200 --> 01:33:47,410
Why should I? A little business meeting...

1059
01:33:48,160 --> 01:33:50,870
To settle the estate of your late wife.

1060
01:33:51,290 --> 01:33:54,920
I don't know what you mean.

1061
01:33:55,000 --> 01:33:59,420
Come on. Quit stalling, Thorwald, or I'll hang up and call the police.

1062
01:33:59,750 --> 01:34:03,050
I have only $100 or so. That's a start.

1063
01:34:03,550 --> 01:34:07,220
I'm at the Albert now. I'll be looking for you.

1064
01:34:26,700 --> 01:34:28,320
Come on, Stella, let's go.

1065
01:34:28,410 --> 01:34:29,990
One of you keep an eye on this window.

1066
01:34:30,080 --> 01:34:33,660
If I see him coming back, I'll signal with a flashbulb.

1067
01:36:08,170 --> 01:36:09,840
This is the Doyle residence.

1068
01:36:09,930 --> 01:36:14,140
Hello, this is L. B. Jefferies speaking. I'm a friend of Mr. Doyle.

1069
01:36:14,430 --> 01:36:18,180
Who's this? This is a babysitter. Oh. Uh...

1070
01:36:18,270 --> 01:36:19,520
When do you expect them in?

1071
01:36:19,600 --> 01:36:24,150
Well, I'm hired till 1:00. They went to dinner and maybe a nightclub.

1072
01:36:24,520 --> 01:36:25,860
I see.

1073
01:36:26,360 --> 01:36:29,990
If he calls in, would you have him get in touch with L. B. Jefferies right away?

1074
01:36:30,070 --> 01:36:31,990
I might have quite a surprise for him.

1075
01:36:32,070 --> 01:36:34,990
Well, do we have your number, Mr. Jefferies?

1076
01:36:35,080 --> 01:36:37,200
He has it. Good night.

1077
01:36:37,290 --> 01:36:38,750
Good night.

1078
01:36:56,720 --> 01:36:59,520
Stella was wrong about Miss Lonelyheart.

1079
01:37:31,380 --> 01:37:33,800
Lisa, what are you... Don't do...

1080
01:37:49,940 --> 01:37:52,610
Lisa, what are you doing? Don't go...

1081
01:38:26,440 --> 01:38:29,070
Come on, come on! Get out of there!

1082
01:38:35,320 --> 01:38:39,200
She said ring Thorwald's phone the second you see him come back.

1083
01:38:39,280 --> 01:38:43,080
I'm gonna ring it right now! Oh, no. Give her another minute.

1084
01:38:46,330 --> 01:38:48,920
All right, fellows, let's try it once from the beginning.

1085
01:38:49,000 --> 01:38:50,750
Miss Lonelyheart.

1086
01:38:50,840 --> 01:38:52,710
Oh, call the police!

1087
01:38:55,090 --> 01:38:58,260
Operator. Get me the police, sixth precinct.

1088
01:38:58,340 --> 01:38:59,680
Yes, sir.

1089
01:39:11,860 --> 01:39:14,570
Mr. Jefferies, the music stopped her.

1090
01:39:22,080 --> 01:39:23,790
What... Lisa!

1091
01:39:26,210 --> 01:39:27,370
Lisa!

1092
01:39:38,630 --> 01:39:40,340
Precinct 6, Sergeant Allgood.

1093
01:39:40,430 --> 01:39:45,600
Hello. Hello. Look, a man is assaulting a woman at 125 West 9th Street.

1094
01:39:45,680 --> 01:39:49,230
Second floor, at the rear. Make it fast!

1095
01:39:49,310 --> 01:39:52,020
Your name? L. B. Jefferies.

1096
01:39:52,110 --> 01:39:55,490
Phone number? Chelsea 25598.

1097
01:40:16,090 --> 01:40:17,760
The door was open.

1098
01:40:39,780 --> 01:40:41,820
- I told you... - Oh, no!

1099
01:40:43,700 --> 01:40:46,990
Let go of me! Jeff! Oh, no!

1100
01:40:47,080 --> 01:40:48,620
Jeff! Jeff!

1101
01:40:49,120 --> 01:40:50,210
Jeff!

1102
01:40:51,750 --> 01:40:52,960
Lisa!

1103
01:40:53,960 --> 01:40:56,550
Stella, what do we do?

1104
01:40:56,630 --> 01:40:57,840
Jeff!

1105
01:40:58,630 --> 01:41:00,880
- Jeff! - Well, here they come.

1106
01:41:50,930 --> 01:41:53,140
What's she trying to do? Why doesn't she turn him in?

1107
01:41:53,230 --> 01:41:56,270
She's a smart girl. "Smart girl"? She'll get herself arrested.

1108
01:41:56,360 --> 01:41:59,030
It'll get her out of there, won't it?

1109
01:42:01,690 --> 01:42:03,610
Look, the wedding ring!

1110
01:42:09,410 --> 01:42:11,870
Turn off the light! He's seen us!

1111
01:42:21,800 --> 01:42:23,470
How long do you think he'll stay there?

1112
01:42:23,550 --> 01:42:27,640
Well, unless he's dumber than I think he is, he won't wait until his lease is up.

1113
01:42:27,720 --> 01:42:29,720
Get my billfold out of the drawer in the table there.

1114
01:42:29,810 --> 01:42:32,680
What do you need money for? I'm gonna bail Lisa out of jail.

1115
01:42:32,770 --> 01:42:35,100
You know, you could leave her there till next Tuesday,

1116
01:42:35,190 --> 01:42:37,650
then you could sneak safely away as planned.

1117
01:42:37,730 --> 01:42:41,650
Yeah, let's see. $127.

1118
01:42:41,730 --> 01:42:42,780
How much do you need?

1119
01:42:42,860 --> 01:42:46,280
Well, this is first-offense burglary, that's about $250.

1120
01:42:46,360 --> 01:42:48,240
- Lisa's handbag. - Yeah.

1121
01:42:49,580 --> 01:42:52,040
How much does she have? Fifty cents.

1122
01:42:52,120 --> 01:42:55,330
Here, take this. Look, I got $20 or so in my purse.

1123
01:42:55,410 --> 01:42:56,830
What about the rest? What about the rest?

1124
01:42:56,920 --> 01:42:59,460
When those cops see Lisa, they'll even contribute.

1125
01:42:59,540 --> 01:43:00,840
Well, hurry up.

1126
01:43:05,090 --> 01:43:07,430
Just a minute. Hurry up!

1127
01:43:08,090 --> 01:43:09,100
Jefferies.

1128
01:43:09,180 --> 01:43:10,850
All right. What is it now?

1129
01:43:10,930 --> 01:43:14,100
Doyle, I've got something really big for you.

1130
01:43:14,180 --> 01:43:16,100
Why did I have to return your call?

1131
01:43:16,190 --> 01:43:19,400
Look, Jeff! Don't louse up my night with another mad killer...

1132
01:43:19,480 --> 01:43:24,070
Listen to me! Listen to me! Lisa's in jail. She got arrested.

1133
01:43:24,440 --> 01:43:26,150
- Your Lisa? - My Lisa.

1134
01:43:26,240 --> 01:43:30,070
Boy, you should have seen her. She got into Thorwald's apartment.

1135
01:43:30,160 --> 01:43:33,330
But then he came back, and the only way I could get her out was to call the police.

1136
01:43:33,410 --> 01:43:35,040
- I told you... - I know you told me!

1137
01:43:35,120 --> 01:43:38,210
She went in to get evidence and she came out with evidence.

1138
01:43:38,290 --> 01:43:39,920
- Like what? - Like what?

1139
01:43:40,000 --> 01:43:42,920
Like Mrs. Thorwald's wedding ring. That's like what.

1140
01:43:43,000 --> 01:43:46,800
If that woman was alive, she'd be wearing that ring. Right?

1141
01:43:47,970 --> 01:43:50,010
- A possibility. - A possibility. It's a fact!

1142
01:43:50,090 --> 01:43:54,100
He killed a dog last night because the dog was scratching around in the garden.

1143
01:43:54,180 --> 01:43:58,350
You know why? Because he had something buried in that garden that the dog scented.

1144
01:43:58,440 --> 01:43:59,560
Like an old ham bone?

1145
01:43:59,650 --> 01:44:02,730
Look, I don't know what pet names Thorwald had for his wife,

1146
01:44:02,820 --> 01:44:04,190
but I'll tell you this,

1147
01:44:04,280 --> 01:44:07,530
all those trips at night with that metal suitcase,

1148
01:44:07,610 --> 01:44:09,240
he wasn't taking out his possessions,

1149
01:44:09,320 --> 01:44:12,740
because his possessions are still up in the apartment.

1150
01:44:12,830 --> 01:44:14,490
Perhaps it was old "ham bone."

1151
01:44:14,580 --> 01:44:16,200
Yeah, in sections.

1152
01:44:16,290 --> 01:44:17,620
And I'll tell you something else.

1153
01:44:17,710 --> 01:44:20,790
All the telephone calls he made were long distance, all right?

1154
01:44:20,880 --> 01:44:24,630
Now, if he called his wife long distance on the day she left,

1155
01:44:24,710 --> 01:44:26,920
after she arrived in Merritsville,

1156
01:44:27,010 --> 01:44:30,260
why did she write a card to him saying that she'd arrived in Merritsville?

1157
01:44:30,340 --> 01:44:32,180
Why did she do that?

1158
01:44:32,930 --> 01:44:35,260
Where'd they take Lisa? Precinct 6.

1159
01:44:35,350 --> 01:44:38,180
I sent somebody over with the bail money. Maybe you won't need it.

1160
01:44:38,270 --> 01:44:41,440
I'll run it down, Jeff. All right. All right. Now hurry up, will you?

1161
01:44:41,520 --> 01:44:43,360
This fellow knows he's being watched.

1162
01:44:43,440 --> 01:44:45,270
He's not gonna wait around forever. Hurry up.

1163
01:44:45,360 --> 01:44:47,360
If that ring checks out, we'll give him an escort.

1164
01:44:47,440 --> 01:44:49,570
- So long, Jeff. - So long.

1165
01:44:57,700 --> 01:45:01,540
Hello. Hello. Tom, I think Thorwald's left. I don't see...

1166
01:45:04,250 --> 01:45:05,630
Hello...

1167
01:47:37,400 --> 01:47:39,410
What do you want from me?

1168
01:47:43,330 --> 01:47:45,410
Your friend, the girl, could have turned me in.

1169
01:47:45,500 --> 01:47:47,040
Why didn't she?

1170
01:47:49,170 --> 01:47:52,960
What is it you want? A lot of money? I don't have any money.

1171
01:47:56,670 --> 01:47:58,260
Say something.

1172
01:48:00,010 --> 01:48:02,930
Say something! Tell me what you want!

1173
01:48:06,060 --> 01:48:08,310
Can you get me that ring back?

1174
01:48:09,480 --> 01:48:10,940
No.

1175
01:48:11,020 --> 01:48:12,860
Tell her to bring it back!

1176
01:48:12,940 --> 01:48:15,070
I can't. The police have it by now.

1177
01:48:52,230 --> 01:48:54,060
Lisa! Doyle!

1178
01:49:02,490 --> 01:49:03,910
Over there!

1179
01:49:09,790 --> 01:49:12,250
Look! Look, over in that apartment!

1180
01:49:31,270 --> 01:49:32,600
Doyle!

1181
01:49:36,690 --> 01:49:38,780
Creele! Give me your .38!

1182
01:49:49,790 --> 01:49:51,580
I'm sorry, Jeff. I got here as fast as I could!

1183
01:49:51,660 --> 01:49:53,420
Oh, Mr. Jefferies! Don't let anybody touch him.

1184
01:49:53,500 --> 01:49:55,330
Get my medical bag from upstairs.

1185
01:49:55,420 --> 01:49:57,540
Oh, Lisa, sweetie, if anything had happened to you...

1186
01:49:57,630 --> 01:49:59,380
Oh, shut up. I'm all right.

1187
01:49:59,460 --> 01:50:01,420
Gee, I'm proud of you!

1188
01:50:02,510 --> 01:50:04,840
You got enough for a search warrant now?

1189
01:50:04,930 --> 01:50:06,100
Oh, yeah, sure.

1190
01:50:06,180 --> 01:50:08,260
- Lieutenant Doyle! - Yeah?

1191
01:50:08,350 --> 01:50:10,560
Is he okay? He's alive.

1192
01:50:10,600 --> 01:50:14,190
Thorwald's ready to take us on a tour of the East River.

1193
01:50:19,530 --> 01:50:21,650
Did he say what was buried in the flower bed?

1194
01:50:21,740 --> 01:50:25,860
Yeah. He said the dog got too inquisitive, so he dug it up.

1195
01:50:26,490 --> 01:50:29,330
It's in a hat box over in his apartment.

1196
01:50:29,830 --> 01:50:30,950
- Want to look? - No, thanks.

1197
01:50:31,040 --> 01:50:33,250
I don't want any part of her.

1198
01:50:39,500 --> 01:50:42,300
I hope it's gonna be a hit. This is the first release.

1199
01:50:42,380 --> 01:50:44,300
I'd love to hear it.

1200
01:50:44,380 --> 01:50:47,720
I can't tell you what this music has meant to me.

1201
01:51:03,150 --> 01:51:05,200
Now, let's try it again, puppy.

1202
01:51:05,280 --> 01:51:07,530
Stay still. Stay.

1203
01:51:15,290 --> 01:51:16,750
- Stanley! - Hi, baby!

1204
01:51:19,840 --> 01:51:22,630
My, look what the Army's done for you!

1205
01:51:24,550 --> 01:51:28,590
The Army's made me hungry. What've you got in the icebox to eat?

1206
01:51:29,260 --> 01:51:31,350
Boy, it's good to be home.

1207
01:51:34,770 --> 01:51:36,350
I don't care if you help me or not,

1208
01:51:36,440 --> 01:51:39,440
but if you'd told me you'd quit your job, we wouldn't have gotten married.

1209
01:51:39,520 --> 01:51:41,440
Oh, honey, come on...

1210
01:52:10,890 --> 01:52:16,140
Lisa

